      ],
      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"Cold start: execution begins here at power-on and reset.\nLoads HL with $7FFF, the highest address the RAM fill writes, and A with $3F, the\nhigh byte the fill stops at, and jumps to RAM_FILL.\nDoes not return.\nChanges: HL, A.","comment":"HL = $7FFF: RAM_FILL writes from here down"},
        "3": {"comment":"A = $3F: the fill stops when H drops to $3F, after $4000 is written"},
        "5": {"comment":"Go on at RAM_FILL"},
        "8": {"synopsis":"RST $08: records an error. The byte after the RST is the error code; the report shown is\nthe code + 1 (RUN_STOP). ERR_NR takes the code only if it still holds a value with bit 7 set\n($FF, no error), so the first error is kept.\nDoes not return to the byte after the RST: it returns from the routine that made it, which\nleaves the work to stop to its callers (RUN_STATEMENT checks ERR_NR).\nChanges: HL.","comment":"HL = the address of the code byte"},
        "9": {"comment":"L = the error code"},
        "10": {"comment":"ERR_NR bit 7: no error recorded yet?"},
        "16": {"synopsis":"RST $10: prints the character in A at the print position (PRINT_CHAR).\nExit: carry set if it was written, clear if the display had no line left.","comment":"Go on at PRINT_CHAR"},
        "19": {"synopsis":"Stores the code unless an error is already recorded.","comment":"An error already: keep it"},
        "20": {"comment":"ERR_NR = the code"},
        "23": {"comment":"Return from the routine that made the RST"},
        "24": {"synopsis":"RST $18: the next character of the line (NEXT_CHAR).","comment":"Go on at NEXT_CHAR"},
        "26": {"synopsis":"A = the character at CH_ADD, or the first one after it that is not a space ($00).\nExit: HL = CH_ADD, A = the character.","comment":"HL = CH_ADD"},
        "30": {"synopsis":"Returns unless the character is a space.","comment":"A space?"},
        "32": {"synopsis":"RST $20: the next character after CH_ADD that is not a space (NEXT_CHAR, then GC_TEST).","comment":"Step on"},
        "37": {"synopsis":"Steps CH_ADD one character on (NC_STEP, with HL = CH_ADD) and evaluates the expression from\nthere (falls into EXPR_RST).","comment":"Step past the current character"},
        "40": {"synopsis":"RST $28: evaluates the expression at CH_ADD (GET_CHAR, then EVAL with B = 0).\nExit: RESULT = the value (a number, or a string's text address); FLAGS bit 6 = its type\n(1 a number, 0 a string). Checking (FLAGS bit 7 clear): only the syntax and the types.","comment":"The first character"},
        "43": {"comment":"B = 0: the stack's bottom priority"},
        "48": {"synopsis":"RST $30: adds BC bytes to the end of the workspace, just below D_FILE (WS_ROOM), if there is\nroom (TEST_ROOM).\nExit: carry set, DE = the first of the bytes, HL = the last; carry clear (and error code\n$03 recorded) when there is no room.","comment":"Room for BC bytes?"},
        "51": {"comment":"No"},
        "56": {"synopsis":"The INT handler (IM 1), part of the picture RUN_DISPLAY makes. The display file runs as code at\nD_FILE + $8000; its characters are NOPs there and each row's $76 a HALT (the core's M1\nrule: a byte with bit 6 clear fetched above 32K reads as a NOP). The ULA raises INT when\nbit 6 of R drops to 0; INT_DISPLAY counts each one as a TV line.\nC counts the lines of the current row, B the rows left, HL holds the row's start, A the R\nvalue a row starts with.\n- More lines in this row: the row runs again from HL (INT_SAME_ROW).\n- Row done: HL = the byte after its HALT (the next row); when B counts down to 0, returns\n  to RUN_DISPLAY's caller; otherwise C = 8 lines for the next row.","comment":"One line of the row done"},
        "57": {"comment":"More lines to go in this row: run it again"},
        "60": {"comment":"The interrupted address, just past the row's HALT: the next row starts there"},
        "61": {"comment":"One row fewer"},
        "62": {"comment":"All rows done: back to RUN_DISPLAY's caller"},
        "63": {"comment":"C = 8: each text row is 8 TV lines"},
        "65": {"synopsis":"Restarts a row: R = A, interrupts on, and the row runs from HL.","comment":"R = A: the next INT comes when R's bit 6 drops"},
        "68": {"comment":"Run the row (the display file, as NOPs up to its HALT)"},
        "69": {"synopsis":"Same row again: drops the interrupted address and restarts the row from HL.","comment":"Drop the address after the HALT"},
        "70": {"comment":"Never taken: Z is clear after the DEC C that came here"},
        "73": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the expression after the '(' at CH_ADD (EXPR_NEXT); the ')' must follow\n(MARK_ERROR otherwise), and NEXT_CHAR steps past it.","comment":"Evaluate after the '('"},
        "76": {"comment":"The character after the expression"},
        "77": {"comment":"A ')'?"},
        "82": {"synopsis":"Steps CH_ADD on to the next character and returns it, passing over the cursor ($B0).\nWhen it meets the cursor, P_PTR = its address, and if FLAGX bit 7 is set, FLAGS bit 2 is\nset: the cursor shows as K.\nExit: HL = CH_ADD, A = the character.","comment":"HL = CH_ADD"},
        "85": {"synopsis":"One character on.","comment":"CH_ADD + 1"},
        "90": {"comment":"The cursor?"},
        "93": {"comment":"P_PTR = the cursor's address"},
        "96": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 7: still where a keyword goes?"},
        "100": {"comment":"No: pass over the cursor"},
        "102": {"comment":"Yes: FLAGS bit 2, the K cursor"},
        "106": {"comment":"Pass over the cursor"},
        "108": {"synopsis":"The key codes, one byte per key, indexed by KEY_DECODE as 5 x row + column. The rows are\nin the order WAIT_KEY scans them (port $FEFE first): SHIFT Z X C V / A S D F G /\nQ W E R T / 1 2 3 4 5 / 0 9 8 7 6 / P O I U Y / NEWLINE L K J H / SPACE . M N B.\nUnshifted, entries 1-39 ($006C-$0092): each key's own character, NEWLINE $76, SPACE $00.\nEntry 0 is SHIFT itself and is never read ($006B is the table's base).\nShifted, entries 40-78, from KEY_TABLE_SHIFT. In K mode KEY_CODE adds $C0 to a letter,\nmaking it a keyword token ($E6-$FF)."},
        "147": {"synopsis":"The shifted key codes, entries 40-78, keys in the same order as KEY_TABLE:\nZ : / X ;($D7) / C ? / V /($DF) / A-G: graphics $09 $08 $06 $07 $0B /\nQ-T: graphics $02 $03 $04 $05 $0A / 1 NOT($DB) / 2 AND($E0) / 3 THEN($D5) / 4 TO($D6) /\n5 $72 / 0 $77 / 9 $74 / 8 $73 / 7 $70 / 6 $71 / P *($DE) / O )($D9) / I (($DA) / U $ / Y \" /\nNEWLINE $75 / L =($E3) / K +($DD) / J -($DC) / H **($E2) / SPACE £ / . ,($D8) / M >($E4) /\nN <($E5) / B OR($E1).\nThe codes $70-$77 are the editing keys (EDIT_KEY_TABLE)."},
        "186": {"synopsis":"The keyword table. $00BA holds $D4, the first token code. From $00BB, 45 spellings in the\ncharacter set, the last character of each with bit 7 set; FIND_TOKEN reaches token\n$D4 + n by counting n + 1 end marks. The first spelling, '?', serves codes below $D4.\n$D4 \" / $D5 THEN / $D6 TO / $D7 ; / $D8 , / $D9 ) / $DA ( / $DB NOT / $DC - / $DD + / $DE * /\n$DF / / $E0 AND / $E1 OR / $E2 ** / $E3 = / $E4 > / $E5 < / $E6 LIST / $E7 RETURN / $E8 CLS /\n$E9 DIM / $EA SAVE / $EB FOR / $EC GO TO / $ED POKE / $EE INPUT / $EF RANDOMISE / $F0 LET /\n$F1 ? / $F2 ? / $F3 NEXT / $F4 PRINT / $F5 ? / $F6 NEW / $F7 RUN / $F8 STOP / $F9 CONTINUE /\n$FA IF / $FB GO SUB / $FC LOAD / $FD CLEAR / $FE REM / $FF ?.\nThe K-mode letters A-Z give $E6-$FF: A LIST ... Z ?."},
        "316": {"synopsis":"The bottom border: runs the display file's last HALT for ($4023) lines, one fewer than\nthe top border (RUN_DISPLAY, one row), then falls into WAIT_KEY for the next frame.","comment":"The bottom border's lines"},
        "319": {"synopsis":"Waits for a key, drawing the picture while it waits: each pass of the loop is one TV frame.\n1. FRAMES + 1. Reads the keyboard, eight rows from port $FEFE up; each IN from port $FE also\n   starts the vertical sync. H gathers the column bits (bit 0 = SHIFT, 1 = not pressed,\n   columns 0-4 in bits 1-5), L the row bits (0 = a key in that row).\n2. ($4023) = the border's line count: $38 when bit 6 of the port reads 1, $20 when it\n   reads 0 (in Klive's core bit 6 is 1 on the 50 Hz machine).\n   ($4022) and ($4023), RESULT's two bytes, serve as the key count and the border count.\n3. Returns when the keys read the same as last frame (kept in CH_ADD), a key other than\n   SHIFT is down, and the count in ($4022) has run out. While a key stays down the count is\n   set to $0F; with none down it halves each frame. So a key is taken once per press, after\n   the keyboard was free for a few frames.\n4. Otherwise ends the sync (OUT ($FF)), shows the top border and the 24 rows\n   (RUN_DISPLAY with B = 25), then the bottom border (WK_BOTTOM), and loops.\nExit: B = the column bits, C = the row bits (KEY_DECODE reads them), DE = 0, HL = $4022.\nNo picture is made while the key is handled.","comment":"A short pause"},
        "321": {"synopsis":"WAIT_KEY's opening pause: 8 DJNZ loops."},
        "323": {"comment":"FRAMES + 1"},
        "330": {"comment":"H and L gather the key bits: all 1, no key yet"},
        "333": {"comment":"B = $FE: the first row, port $FEFE"},
        "336": {"comment":"Read the row; this also starts the vertical sync"},
        "338": {"comment":"Bit 0 of the first row is SHIFT: left out here, read at the end"},
        "340": {"synopsis":"One row: H &= its column bits; L keeps the row's bit (0) if a key in it is down.","comment":"Only bits 0-4 are keys"},
        "343": {"comment":"A = the keys down, as 1s"},
        "344": {"comment":"Carry if none"},
        "346": {"comment":"A = $FF if none, else 0"},
        "347": {"comment":"Or B: the row's own 0 bit stays"},
        "349": {"comment":"L: the row bits so far"},
        "351": {"comment":"H: the column bits so far"},
        "353": {"comment":"Next row; carry until all eight are read"},
        "359": {"comment":"Port $FEFE again: bit 0, SHIFT, into the carry"},
        "360": {"comment":"SHIFT into H bit 0; the columns move to bits 1-5"},
        "365": {"comment":"A = $FF if bit 6 of the port read 1, else 0"},
        "368": {"comment":"A = $38 or $20"},
        "370": {"comment":"($4023): the border's line count for this frame"},
        "373": {"comment":"BC = last frame's keys"},
        "377": {"comment":"Keep this frame's in CH_ADD"},
        "380": {"comment":"Carry if last frame had no key but SHIFT (B was $FE or $FF)"},
        "383": {"comment":"Zero only if the keys are the same and one was down last frame"},
        "386": {"comment":"($4022): the count since the keyboard was last free"},
        "392": {"comment":"Same key two frames running and the count out: return it"},
        "393": {"comment":"A = $FF if a key was down last frame"},
        "399": {"comment":"A key down: count = $0F; none: count halves"},
        "403": {"comment":"B = $1E for the pause"},
        "404": {"synopsis":"A pause before the sync ends."},
        "406": {"comment":"End the vertical sync"},
        "408": {"comment":"A: the first row's R, so INT comes after 20 fetches"},
        "410": {"comment":"25 rows: the top border, then the 24 text rows"},
        "412": {"comment":"The display file, run at D_FILE + $8000"},
        "417": {"comment":"The top border (($4023) lines of D_FILE's HALT) and the text rows"},
        "420": {"comment":"A: the bottom border's R"},
        "422": {"comment":"B = 1: one row"},
        "423": {"comment":"Back to the last row's HALT"},
        "424": {"comment":"The bottom border has one line fewer"},
        "427": {"comment":"The bottom border, then the next frame"},
        "429": {"synopsis":"Runs the display file from HL (an address + $8000) with interrupts on; INT_DISPLAY counts\nthe lines. The first row has ($4023) lines, the others 8.\nEntry: HL = the row to start at, A = R for the first row, B = the rows.\nReturns, from INT_DISPLAY, when the B rows are done, with HL = the address after the\nlast row's HALT.","comment":"C = the first row's lines"},
        "432": {"comment":"R = A: when the first INT comes"},
        "434": {"comment":"A = $DD: each later row's R, INT after 35 fetches"},
        "437": {"comment":"Run the display file"},
        "438": {"synopsis":"SAVE (class 0): writes memory from $4000 up to E_LINE - 1 (the system variables, the\nprogram and the variables) to tape, then starts a new edit line; a run ends here. The tape\noutput is the vertical sync: an IN from port $FE switches it on, an OUT off (Klive's core\nREADME).\n1. A leader: $12CB passes of an IN and a pause, the output held on.\n2. Each byte from its top bit: a train of pulses, 9 for a 1 and 4 for a 0, then a gap.\nSPACE (BREAK) stops it at any time (TAPE_DONE). No report is printed.","comment":"Drop LINE_SCAN's return"},
        "439": {"comment":"The leader's length"},
        "442": {"synopsis":"One pass of the leader: the output on, BREAK tested.","comment":"Row $7FFE: SPACE is bit 0"},
        "444": {"comment":"Also switches the output on"},
        "447": {"comment":"SPACE: stop"},
        "449": {"synopsis":"A pause."},
        "451": {"comment":"Count the passes"},
        "456": {"synopsis":"The data, from $4000.","comment":"HL = $4000"},
        "459": {"synopsis":"A byte: E = 8 bits; D = the first gap's length.","comment":"D = $F8, E = 8"},
        "462": {"synopsis":"One bit, the byte's top one: C = 9 pulses for a 1, 4 for a 0 (eight RLCs leave the byte\nas it was).","comment":"The bit into the carry"},
        "464": {"comment":"A = $FF for a 1"},
        "467": {"comment":"C = 9 or 4"},
        "470": {"synopsis":"One pulse: the output off, a pause, on, a pause.","comment":"Off"},
        "472": {"comment":"36 loops"},
        "474": {"synopsis":"The pause with the output off."},
        "478": {"comment":"On again (and BREAK read into A)"},
        "480": {"comment":"35 loops"},
        "482": {"synopsis":"The pause with the output on."},
        "484": {"comment":"Count the pulses"},
        "487": {"comment":"The gap after the bit: D loops"},
        "488": {"synopsis":"The gap between bits.","comment":"A slower loop"},
        "491": {"comment":"Later gaps: $FE loops"},
        "493": {"comment":"Count the bits"},
        "496": {"comment":"SPACE (from the last IN): stop"},
        "499": {"comment":"The next byte, or the end"},
        "504": {"synopsis":"HL + 1; returns while it is below E_LINE, with DE = the bytes left - 1. At E_LINE drops\nthe return address and ends the transfer (TAPE_DONE). SAVE's and LOAD's.","comment":"Next address"},
        "510": {"comment":"E_LINE - 1 - HL: carry once HL reaches E_LINE"},
        "513": {"comment":"Not yet: go on"},
        "514": {"comment":"Done: drop the return"},
        "515": {"synopsis":"The end of SAVE or LOAD, finished or broken: a new edit line (EDIT_RESET)."},
        "518": {"synopsis":"LOAD (class 0): reads memory from $4000 on from tape, up to the E_LINE the tape itself\nbrings (its bytes $0A-$0B), then starts a new edit line; a run ends here. The tape input is\nbit 7 of port $FE.\n1. Waits for $5712 reads in a row with no signal (LD_QUIET).\n2. E_LINE's high byte + 1, so NEXT_TAPE_BYTE cannot stop the load early while E_LINE\n   itself is coming in.\n3. Each bit is a train of pulses; LD_COUNT counts the reads it lasts.\nSPACE (BREAK) stops it (LD_BREAK).\nObserved (hello.o, real-time load): 1 bits ended with C = $D6-$D7 (about 189 reads), 0\nbits with C = $37-$38 (about 93); the bytes matched the file.","comment":"Drop LINE_SCAN's return"},
        "519": {"synopsis":"Starts the count of quiet reads again.","comment":"$5712 reads"},
        "522": {"synopsis":"One read: SPACE stops; a signal (bit 7) starts the count again.","comment":"Row $7FFE"},
        "527": {"comment":"SPACE: stop"},
        "529": {"comment":"Bit 7, the tape, into the carry"},
        "531": {"comment":"A signal: not quiet yet"},
        "533": {"comment":"Count the quiet reads"},
        "538": {"comment":"E_LINE's high byte + 1 while E_LINE loads"},
        "541": {"comment":"From $4000"},
        "544": {"synopsis":"A byte: 8 bits.","comment":"E = 8"},
        "546": {"synopsis":"Waits for a pulse train to start; SPACE stops.","comment":"Row $7FFE"},
        "551": {"comment":"SPACE: stop"},
        "555": {"comment":"No signal yet: wait"},
        "557": {"comment":"C counts the reads, from $94"},
        "559": {"synopsis":"A signal: 26 more quiet reads are needed to end the train.","comment":"B = 26"},
        "561": {"synopsis":"Counts the train's reads in C (down from $94, wrapping past 0) until 26 quiet reads in a\nrow. Then by C: bit 7 set (fewer than 21 reads, or more than 148) is a 1; below $56 (63 to\n148 reads) a 0; $56-$7F (21 to 62 reads) is not a bit, and the wait starts again.","comment":"One read more"},
        "564": {"comment":"Carry = the tape bit"},
        "565": {"comment":"Z: C's bit 7 clear"},
        "568": {"comment":"A signal: B again"},
        "570": {"comment":"Count the quiet reads"},
        "572": {"comment":"C's bit 7 set: a 1 (carry clear here)"},
        "574": {"comment":"Below $56: a 0 (carry set)"},
        "576": {"comment":"Neither: wait for the next train"},
        "578": {"synopsis":"Shifts the bit into the byte at HL.","comment":"The carry is the bit inverted: flip it"},
        "579": {"comment":"Into the byte"},
        "581": {"comment":"Count the bits"},
        "584": {"comment":"The next byte, or the end at E_LINE"},
        "589": {"synopsis":"SPACE during the data: with D (the high byte of the bytes left - 1, from NEXT_TAPE_BYTE) 1 or more, a\ncold restart (START), memory being half loaded; otherwise E_LINE's high byte is put back\nand a new edit line started.","comment":"D - 1: positive when D was 1 or more"},
        "593": {"comment":"E_LINE's high byte back"},
        "598": {"synopsis":"LIST [n]: E_PPC = n with its top two bits cleared (0 without n), and a new edit line\n(EDIT_RESET), from which MAIN_LOOP lists. Drops LINE_SCAN's return, so a run ends here\nwithout a report.","comment":"Bits 15-14 off"},
        "602": {"comment":"E_PPC = n"},
        "606": {"comment":"Drop the return: leave the run"},
        "609": {"synopsis":"Start-up after START: sizes the RAM, sets up the stack, the CPU's I, interrupt mode\nand IY, and the first system variables, then falls into EDIT_RESET and the main loop.\nEntry: HL = $7FFF, A = $3F (from START).\n1. Writes $01 from HL down to $4000.\n2. Walks up from $4000 counting each byte down once (RAM_CHECK); the first byte that\n   does not become 0 is the first address past the RAM.\n3. SP = that address; I = $0E, IM 1, IY = $4000.\n4. VARS = $4028 with a variables area of one $80 byte; E_LINE = $4029.\nDoes not return.","comment":"Write $01: RAM_CHECK counts it down to 0"},
        "611": {"comment":"Next address down"},
        "612": {"comment":"Has H dropped to $3F (HL below $4000)?"},
        "613": {"comment":"No: keep filling"},
        "615": {"synopsis":"Walks up from $4000 counting each byte down once. A RAM byte holds RAM_FILL's $01\nand becomes 0, and the walk goes on. It stops at the first byte that does not become\n0: no memory there, or a copy of $4000 (already counted down to 0) where the RAM\nrepeats above its top. Klive's ZX80 repeats its RAM: the runs stop at $4400 on the\n1K machine and at $8000 on the 16K one.\nExit: HL = the first address past the RAM; the RAM below it is all 0.","comment":"The next address up"},
        "616": {"comment":"$01 becomes 0 only in RAM. At a copy of $4000 this makes ERR_NR $FF, its 'no report' value (both runs)"},
        "617": {"comment":"0: this byte is RAM, check the next one"},
        "619": {"comment":"SP = the first address past the RAM: the stack grows down from the top of RAM"},
        "620": {"comment":"AF (A = $3F) becomes the stack's bottom word, which RETURN_CMD recognises by its $3F"},
        "623": {"comment":"I = $0E: the ULA fetches character patterns from $0E00"},
        "625": {"comment":"Interrupt mode 1: INT goes to $0038"},
        "627": {"comment":"IY = $4000: (IY+n) addresses the system variables from now on"},
        "631": {"comment":"HL = $4028, the first byte after the system variables"},
        "634": {"comment":"VARS = $4028: the variables area starts there"},
        "637": {"comment":"$80 at VARS: the variables area holds only this byte"},
        "640": {"comment":"E_LINE = $4029: the edit line follows the variables area"},
        "643": {"synopsis":"Puts an empty edit line at E_LINE: the cursor ($B0, an inverse K) and the $76 that\nends a line. The display file starts right after it (D_FILE), the lower part of the\nscreen gets two lines (DF_SZ = 2), and EDIT_RESET falls into MAIN_LOOP.\nThe end of start-up. Also entered by a jump from $0203 and $025F, from KEY_EDIT (no line\nto copy), KEY_NEWLINE (an empty line), RUN_STOP and SL_DONE.","comment":"HL = E_LINE"},
        "646": {"comment":"The cursor: $B0, an inverse K"},
        "649": {"comment":"$76 ends the line"},
        "652": {"comment":"D_FILE = the byte after the edit line"},
        "655": {"comment":"DF_SZ = 2: two lines in the lower part of the screen"},
        "659": {"synopsis":"The main loop, one pass per change to the listing:\n1. Empties the display file (CLS_DISPLAY). When the lower part leaves room, lists the\n   program from S_TOP (moved to E_PPC if that is above it), the current line (E_PPC) marked\n   with an inverse '>' (LIST_LINE). When the current line did not fit, moves S_TOP\n   and lists again.\n2. Shows the edit line in the lower part of the screen (EDIT_LINE_SHOW), growing the\n   lower part until it fits.\n3. Waits for a key (WAIT_KEY), decodes it (KEY_DECODE) and inserts it at the cursor or\n   runs its editing action (EDIT_KEY_TABLE).\nThe editing actions return to EDIT_LINE_SHOW, or here when the listing must change.\nEntered by falling out of EDIT_RESET, and from REDRAW, SET_LINE_NO and $0405.","comment":"Empty the display file"},
        "662": {"comment":"DE = where the display file goes on"},
        "663": {"comment":"23 - DF_SZ: the lines the listing has, less one"},
        "667": {"comment":"No room for a listing: show the edit line"},
        "670": {"comment":"B = 24 - DF_SZ lines; C = 33"},
        "671": {"comment":"B, C and DE become the print registers"},
        "672": {"comment":"Is E_PPC above S_TOP?"},
        "682": {"comment":"No: list from S_TOP"},
        "684": {"comment":"Yes: S_TOP = E_PPC"},
        "688": {"synopsis":"Lists the program from the line S_TOP finds (HL = S_TOP).","comment":"HL = the first line to list"},
        "691": {"comment":"E = 0: no line before the current one yet"},
        "693": {"synopsis":"Lists lines while they fit and the program lasts.","comment":"One line"},
        "698": {"comment":"E = 1: lines before the current one were listed, and the current one did not fit"},
        "699": {"comment":"Otherwise the listing is done"},
        "701": {"comment":"Keep where the listing stopped"},
        "702": {"comment":"The current line, and the one before it (DE)"},
        "709": {"comment":"Did the listing stop before the line before the current one?"},
        "715": {"comment":"No: scroll on by one line"},
        "717": {"comment":"Yes: S_TOP = that line's number (high byte first in the line)"},
        "723": {"synopsis":"Back to MAIN_LOOP to list again."},
        "725": {"synopsis":"SHIFT 6 (code $71): makes the line after the current one current (E_PPC), staying on the\nlast line at the end of the program, and lists again (SET_LINE_NO).","comment":"HL = E_PPC"},
        "728": {"synopsis":"Reads the line number in the word at HL, finds the line after it and stores that line's\nnumber in the same word (SET_LINE_NO). Entered with HL = S_TOP to scroll the listing by\none line, and with HL = E_PPC (KEY_DOWN).","comment":"DE = the number in the word"},
        "731": {"comment":"Keep the variable's high-byte address"},
        "733": {"comment":"The first line numbered above it"},
        "737": {"comment":"DE = its number, or the last line's at the end"},
        "741": {"synopsis":"Stores DE in the line-number variable whose high byte is at HL (E_PPC or S_TOP) and lists\nagain. With FLAGX bit 5 set it stores nothing and goes straight to EDIT_LINE_SHOW.","comment":"FLAGX bit 5 set?"},
        "747": {"comment":"Store DE, high byte first"},
        "752": {"synopsis":"The listing fits: empties the upper part's remaining lines (FILL_NEWLINES); the lower part\nstarts after them (DF_EA).","comment":"Close the listing's lines"},
        "755": {"comment":"DF_EA = where the lower part starts"},
        "759": {"synopsis":"Shows the edit line in the lower part of the screen, then waits for a key:\n1. Scans the edit line (LINE_SCAN), which finds the cursor (P_PTR) and its mode.\n2. Prints the edit line from DF_EA, DF_SZ lines (LIST_TEXT). When it does not fit, DF_SZ\n   grows by one and the screen is made again (REDRAW), up to 24 lines.\n3. Empties the lower part's remaining lines (FILL_NEWLINES) and waits (WAIT_KEY).\nEntered after an editing key (EDIT_AGAIN) and when the listing is done.","comment":"FLAGS = $01: bit 0 set, K/L (bit 2) cleared"},
        "766": {"comment":"Scan the edit line"},
        "769": {"comment":"DE = DF_EA, the lower part"},
        "773": {"comment":"B = DF_SZ lines"},
        "776": {"comment":"C = 1: the first character starts a new line (the $76 that ends the upper part)"},
        "778": {"comment":"The print registers"},
        "782": {"comment":"Print the edit line"},
        "785": {"comment":"It fitted"},
        "790": {"comment":"One more line for the lower part"},
        "794": {"comment":"Up to 24: make the screen again"},
        "796": {"comment":"DF_SZ = 24: show what fits"},
        "797": {"synopsis":"The edit line is shown: closes the lower part's lines, then waits for a key.","comment":"Empty the remaining lines; DF_END"},
        "800": {"comment":"Wait for a key, drawing the picture meanwhile"},
        "803": {"synopsis":"Turns WAIT_KEY's bits into a key code from KEY_TABLE.\nThe index is 5 x row + column, plus 39 with SHIFT: A starts at -6 or 33, the row pass adds 5\nfor each row up to the key's, the column pass 1 for each column. A press of keys in more\nthan one row or column is not taken: back to EDIT_LINE_SHOW.\nIn K mode (FLAGS bit 2) a letter becomes its keyword token (+ $C0).","comment":"Carry = SHIFT's bit (1 = not pressed)"},
        "805": {"comment":"A = $FF without SHIFT, 0 with"},
        "806": {"comment":"A = $FF or $26"},
        "808": {"comment":"L = 5: the row pass"},
        "810": {"comment":"A = -6 or 33"},
        "811": {"synopsis":"Adds L for each bit of C until the 0 bit (the pressed row, or column).","comment":"+ L for this row or column"},
        "813": {"comment":"Shift the next bit out; 1s come in at the top"},
        "815": {"comment":"Not this one: count on"},
        "817": {"comment":"C = $FF only if no other bit was 0: exactly one key"},
        "818": {"comment":"More than one: ignore the press"},
        "820": {"comment":"Now the columns, from B"},
        "821": {"comment":"After the row pass (L = 5) ..."},
        "822": {"comment":"... L = 1 for the column pass"},
        "824": {"comment":"Count the columns"},
        "826": {"comment":"KEY_TABLE's base (entry 0)"},
        "831": {"comment":"A = the key's code"},
        "832": {"comment":"K mode?"},
        "838": {"comment":"A letter + $C0 is a keyword token"},
        "840": {"comment":"$E6 and up: it was a letter"},
        "844": {"comment":"Not a letter: the plain code"},
        "845": {"synopsis":"A = the key's code. $40-$7F: an editing key (EDIT_KEY_JUMP). Anything else, a character\nor a token, is inserted at the cursor as one byte.","comment":"$40-$7F: overflow flag set"},
        "850": {"comment":"HL = P_PTR, the cursor"},
        "853": {"comment":"One byte of room there"},
        "859": {"comment":"The key's code goes before the cursor"},
        "860": {"synopsis":"Shows the edit line again (EDIT_LINE_SHOW). Where the editing keys end."},
        "862": {"synopsis":"Runs code A's editing routine from EDIT_KEY_TABLE ($0292 + 2A for A = $70-$77), with\nHL = P_PTR, the cursor's address.","comment":"DE = the code (D = 0)"},
        "866": {"comment":"HL = $0292 + 2A, the code's entry"},
        "871": {"comment":"The routine's address on the stack ..."},
        "872": {"comment":"... HL = the cursor ..."},
        "875": {"comment":"... and go there"},
        "876": {"synopsis":"Removes the byte at HL (RECLAIM with BC = 1).","comment":"One byte"},
        "882": {"synopsis":"The editing keys' routines, one word per code $70-$77. EDIT_KEY_JUMP reads code n's at\n$0292 + 2n. The keys, from KEY_TABLE_SHIFT: $70 SHIFT 7 KEY_UP, $71 SHIFT 6 KEY_DOWN,\n$72 SHIFT 5 KEY_LEFT, $73 SHIFT 8 KEY_RIGHT, $74 SHIFT 9 KEY_HOME, $75 SHIFT NEWLINE\nKEY_EDIT, $76 NEWLINE KEY_NEWLINE, $77 SHIFT 0 KEY_RUBOUT. No key gives a code $40-$6F,\nwhich would read code as a table entry."},
        "898": {"synopsis":"SHIFT 5 (code $72): moves the cursor one place left, by swapping it with the byte before\nit. Nothing when the cursor is the edit line's first byte.\nEntry: HL = P_PTR.","comment":"Cursor first? Then nothing"},
        "901": {"comment":"HL = P_PTR - 1 (the DEC, DEC, INC leave one down)"},
        "903": {"synopsis":"SHIFT 8 (code $73): moves the cursor one place right, by swapping it with the byte after\nit. Nothing when that byte is the $76 that ends the line.\nAlso KEY_LEFT's tail, with HL one below the cursor.\nEntry: HL = P_PTR.","comment":"The byte to swap with"},
        "907": {"comment":"The line's end: nothing to do"},
        "909": {"comment":"The cursor goes there ..."},
        "914": {"comment":"... and the byte where the cursor was"},
        "917": {"synopsis":"SHIFT 0 (code $77): removes the byte before the cursor (RECLAIM_1). Nothing when the\ncursor is the edit line's first byte.\nEntry: HL = P_PTR.","comment":"Cursor first? Then nothing"},
        "920": {"comment":"The byte before the cursor"},
        "926": {"synopsis":"Returns unless the cursor ($B0) is the edit line's first byte. If it is, drops the return\naddress and goes back to EDIT_AGAIN: the key does nothing.","comment":"DE = E_LINE"},
        "934": {"comment":"Drop the caller's return"},
        "935": {"synopsis":"An editing key with nothing to do: back to EDIT_AGAIN."},
        "937": {"synopsis":"SHIFT 7 (code $70): makes the line before the current one current (E_PPC) and lists\nagain (SET_E_PPC).","comment":"HL = E_PPC"},
        "940": {"comment":"The current line, and the one before it (DE)"},
        "943": {"comment":"HL = the line before"},
        "944": {"comment":"DE = its number"},
        "947": {"synopsis":"E_PPC = DE, then list again (SET_LINE_NO).","comment":"HL = E_PPC's high byte"},
        "953": {"synopsis":"SHIFT 9 (code $74): E_PPC = 0, so the program's first line becomes current; lists again.","comment":"DE = 0"},
        "958": {"synopsis":"LINE_NO's fallback: tries the line at DE instead, with DE = $03BA, whose two ROM bytes\nare $00 $00, as the last resort.","comment":"HL = the line before"},
        "959": {"comment":"$03BA holds $00 $00: the number 0"},
        "962": {"synopsis":"DE = the number of the line at HL. When HL is not at a line (bits 7-6 of its first byte not\n00: the end of the program) takes the line at DE, the one before; with none, DE = 0.\nEntry: HL = an address in the program, DE = the line before it (as FIND_LINE leaves them).\nExit: DE = the number; HL = the number's second byte.","comment":"A line starts with bits 7-6 = 00"},
        "965": {"comment":"Not a line: the one before"},
        "967": {"comment":"DE = the number, high byte first"},
        "971": {"synopsis":"SHIFT NEWLINE (code $75): copies the current line into the edit line, replacing what was\nthere: its number in four columns (leading zeros as spaces, PRINT_LINE_NO writing at E_LINE\ninstead of the display), the cursor, then its text and $76. The display file starts after\nit and MAIN_LOOP makes the screen again.\nWithout room (34 bytes to spare below the stack) the text is not copied. With no program\n(LINE_NO gives 0), a new edit line (EDIT_RESET).","comment":"C = 0: the first character starts a line"},
        "973": {"comment":"DE = E_LINE: the print position for the number"},
        "977": {"comment":"These become the print registers"},
        "978": {"comment":"The current line (or the next, or the last)"},
        "984": {"comment":"DE = its number"},
        "989": {"comment":"Number 0: no line to edit"},
        "992": {"comment":"HL = the line's start"},
        "993": {"comment":"Write the number at E_LINE"},
        "996": {"comment":"The line's start again"},
        "997": {"comment":"BC = its length"},
        "1000": {"comment":"HL = its text"},
        "1002": {"comment":"BC = the text and its $76"},
        "1004": {"comment":"The print position, past the number ..."},
        "1007": {"comment":"... in DE"},
        "1008": {"comment":"The cursor after the number"},
        "1013": {"comment":"Room for the text and 34 bytes more below the stack?"},
        "1020": {"comment":"No: nothing"},
        "1023": {"comment":"Copy the text and $76"},
        "1025": {"comment":"D_FILE = after the new edit line"},
        "1029": {"comment":"Make the screen again"},
        "1032": {"synopsis":"NEWLINE (code $76): enters the edit line. Nothing while it has a syntax error (X_PTR set).\nRemoves the cursor, then by what the line starts with (GET_CHAR, READ_NUMBER):\n- A line number: stores the line in the program (STORE_LINE).\n- No number: runs it as a command (RUN_START), with PPC = $FFFE; an empty line just\n  starts a new edit line (EDIT_RESET).\nWith FLAGX bit 5 set (observed: while INPUT waits for its answer) the line runs at once,\nnumber or not.","comment":"X_PTR: an error marked?"},
        "1037": {"comment":"Yes: ignore the key"},
        "1039": {"comment":"Remove the cursor"},
        "1045": {"comment":"CH_ADD = E_LINE, the line's start"},
        "1051": {"comment":"Its first character, spaces passed"},
        "1054": {"comment":"FLAGX bit 5: an INPUT answer?"},
        "1060": {"comment":"Read a line number (HL' = 0 if none)"},
        "1063": {"comment":"The number into HL"},
        "1066": {"comment":"A number: store the line"},
        "1069": {"comment":"PPC = $FFFE: the edit line is running"},
        "1074": {"comment":"Empty the display for the command's output"},
        "1077": {"comment":"HL = the character after the (absent) number"},
        "1081": {"comment":"An empty line: a new edit line"},
        "1084": {"synopsis":"Runs the line at HL, and then the program from PPC while statements jump or go on to the\nnext line: ERR_NR = $FF, FLAGS = $88 (bit 7: running, so LINE_SCAN carries out what it\nscans and printing works).","comment":"ERR_NR = $FF: no error"},
        "1088": {"comment":"FLAGS = $88: bit 7, running; bit 3, go to line PPC next"},
        "1092": {"synopsis":"Carries out the line at HL (LINE_SCAN, FLAGS bit 7 set), removes the workspace\n(CLEAR_E_LINE) and decides what runs next:\n- ERR_NR set: stop (RUN_STOP).\n- FLAGS bit 3 set (RUN_START sets it; GOTO_CMD sets it with a new PPC): the line FIND_LINE\n  finds for PPC, or PPC + 1 after an INPUT answer; a PPC of $4000 or more stops, so a\n  command that does not jump ends after itself.\n- Otherwise the line after CH_ADD's.\nStops at the end of the program, and when SPACE is down (row $7FFE, bit 0) before a line.\nPPC = the number of each line run.","comment":"Carry out the line"},
        "1095": {"comment":"Remove E_LINE's bytes up to D_FILE"},
        "1098": {"comment":"DE = PPC"},
        "1105": {"comment":"An INPUT answer?"},
        "1109": {"comment":"Yes: clear it; DE = PPC + 1, the line after the INPUT"},
        "1112": {"synopsis":"Stops on an error; otherwise finds the next line.","comment":"ERR_NR bit 7 clear: an error"},
        "1121": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 3: a jump to PPC asked for? (and clear it)"},
        "1125": {"comment":"HL = the byte after CH_ADD's: the next line"},
        "1129": {"comment":"No jump: run that line"},
        "1131": {"comment":"HL = the line asked for"},
        "1133": {"comment":"$4000 and up ($FFFE, the edit line): stop"},
        "1137": {"comment":"Find it"},
        "1140": {"synopsis":"HL = the line to run; stops when it is past the program (bits 7-6 not 00).\nOtherwise PPC = its number and, unless SPACE is down, RUN_STATEMENT runs its text.","comment":"A line?"},
        "1143": {"comment":"No: the end of the program"},
        "1148": {"comment":"PPC = the line's number"},
        "1152": {"comment":"HL = its text"},
        "1153": {"comment":"Read row $7FFE: SPACE is bit 0"},
        "1158": {"comment":"Not down: run the line"},
        "1160": {"synopsis":"Ends a run: closes the output's last line and fills the screen's remaining lines\n(FILL_NEWLINES), then prints the report on the screen's last line: the report number (ERR_NR + 1,\n0 when no error), '/', and PPC as a signed number (-2 for a command). With an error,\nOLDPPC = PPC, or PPC + 1 for report 9. Then waits for a key (WAIT_KEY), which is not\nkept, and starts a new edit line.","comment":"The print registers from DF_EA and S_POSN"},
        "1163": {"comment":"Close the output's lines"},
        "1166": {"comment":"One line of 32 columns for the report"},
        "1170": {"comment":"A = ERR_NR"},
        "1177": {"comment":"A = the report number; 0 when ERR_NR is $FF"},
        "1178": {"comment":"No error: OLDPPC stays"},
        "1180": {"comment":"Report 9?"},
        "1184": {"comment":"Then OLDPPC = PPC + 1"},
        "1185": {"synopsis":"OLDPPC = BC; BC back to PPC for the report.","comment":"OLDPPC = BC"},
        "1191": {"comment":"Report 9: show PPC itself"},
        "1192": {"synopsis":"Prints the report: its number, '/', then PPC.","comment":"The report number"},
        "1195": {"comment":"'/'"},
        "1198": {"comment":"PPC, signed"},
        "1204": {"comment":"Show the screen until a key"},
        "1207": {"comment":"A new edit line"},
        "1210": {"synopsis":"Stores the edit line in the program as line HL (E_PPC = HL), replacing a line with that\nnumber; a number with no text deletes the line. The display file is emptied first (MAIN_LOOP\nmakes it again). A line that does not fit (TEST_ROOM) is not stored, though the old line\nwith its number is already removed.\nThe stored line: the number (high byte first), the text, $76.","comment":"E_PPC = the number: it becomes the current line"},
        "1214": {"comment":"DE = the text after the number"},
        "1215": {"comment":"Empty the display; HL = D_FILE + 1"},
        "1218": {"comment":"HL = D_FILE + 1 - DE: the text and its $76, + 1"},
        "1221": {"comment":"Where the line goes (Z: one with this number)"},
        "1225": {"comment":"None: nothing to remove"},
        "1227": {"comment":"The old line's length ..."},
        "1230": {"comment":"... and remove it"},
        "1233": {"synopsis":"BC = the new line's size: two number bytes, the text and the $76.","comment":"The size registers"},
        "1234": {"comment":"+ 1: BC = the size"},
        "1237": {"comment":"Size 3: no text, so nothing to store"},
        "1241": {"comment":"Otherwise: room for BC bytes?"},
        "1245": {"comment":"No text, or no room: done"},
        "1248": {"comment":"Open BC bytes at the place (from one below, so a pointer at the place moves too)"},
        "1252": {"comment":"DE = the gap's last byte"},
        "1253": {"comment":"HL = the edit line's $76 (moved)"},
        "1258": {"comment":"BC - 1 bytes: the text and $76, and one byte the number then overwrites"},
        "1259": {"comment":"Copy the text in"},
        "1261": {"comment":"The number, high byte first, at the line's start"},
        "1268": {"synopsis":"A new edit line (EDIT_RESET)."},
        "1271": {"synopsis":"Lists the line at HL: its number (4 columns, PRINT_LINE_NO), an inverse '>' ($97) if it is\nthe current line (E_PPC) or a space, and its text (LIST_TEXT).\nEntry: HL = a line, or the end of the program; the print registers set.\nExit: carry set if the line was listed whole, HL = the next line; carry clear at the end of\nthe program (nothing printed) or when the display ran out of lines.\nE: 1 after a line before the current one, 0 after one past it; unchanged by the current line.","comment":"BC = E_PPC"},
        "1275": {"comment":"Compare this line's number with it"},
        "1278": {"comment":"D = $97, an inverse '>', for the current line"},
        "1282": {"comment":"Other lines: D = $00, a space"},
        "1285": {"comment":"E = 1 if this line is before the current one"},
        "1287": {"synopsis":"Prints the line number unless HL is past the program.","comment":"Below $40: a line (above: the variables area)"},
        "1290": {"comment":"Print the number"},
        "1293": {"comment":"End of the program, or no room"},
        "1294": {"comment":"HL = the line's text"},
        "1296": {"comment":"The '>' or the space"},
        "1298": {"synopsis":"Prints a line's text from HL up to and including its $76: tokens spelled out\n(PRINT_TOKEN), the cursor ($B0) as an inverse K, or an inverse L ($B1) when FLAGS bit 2 is\nclear, and an inverse S ($B8) before the byte at X_PTR. FLAGS bit 0 is set first, so a\nkeyword at the start needs no space before it.\nExit: carry set if all of it fitted (HL = after the $76), clear if the display ran out of\nlines.","comment":"No space needed before a keyword yet"},
        "1302": {"synopsis":"One byte of the text.","comment":"BC = X_PTR"},
        "1309": {"comment":"Not at X_PTR"},
        "1311": {"comment":"An inverse S marks X_PTR"},
        "1314": {"comment":"No room left"},
        "1315": {"synopsis":"Prints the byte at HL by its kind.","comment":"HL back"},
        "1318": {"comment":"The cursor?"},
        "1322": {"comment":"$40-$7F (the $76): print it, and the text ends"},
        "1327": {"comment":"Below $C0: a character"},
        "1329": {"comment":"$C0 and up: a token, spelled out"},
        "1334": {"synopsis":"A character, $00-$3F or inverse $80-$BF."},
        "1337": {"synopsis":"Stops when the display is full, else the next byte.","comment":"No room: carry clear"},
        "1340": {"synopsis":"The cursor: $B0 (inverse K) in K mode, $B1 (inverse L) otherwise.","comment":"K mode?"},
        "1346": {"comment":"No: $B1, an inverse L"},
        "1347": {"synopsis":"Prints the cursor and goes on."},
        "1350": {"synopsis":"A leading zero: printed as E ($00 a space, $1C '0' once a digit was printed), or not at all\nwhen E = $FF.","comment":"A = E"},
        "1351": {"comment":"RLCA then RRCA: A unchanged, carry = E's bit 7"},
        "1353": {"comment":"E = $FF: print nothing"},
        "1356": {"synopsis":"One decimal place of HL: the digit is how often BC (minus the place's value) can be added;\nHL keeps the rest. Prints the digit (PRINT_DIGIT), or PD_ZERO for a 0.\nEntry: HL = the number, BC = -1000, -100 or -10, E = the leading-zero character.\nExit: carry set if printed (or skipped), clear if no room.","comment":"A counts"},
        "1357": {"synopsis":"Adds BC while HL stays 0 or more.","comment":"HL - the place's value"},
        "1361": {"comment":"Undo the last one"},
        "1363": {"comment":"A = the digit"},
        "1364": {"comment":"0: a leading zero, or a '0'"},
        "1366": {"synopsis":"Prints the digit in A. From now on zeros print as '0' (E = $1C).","comment":"E = $1C, '0'"},
        "1368": {"comment":"The digit's character"},
        "1369": {"synopsis":"Prints A (PRINT_CHAR). Any character but a space ($00) clears FLAGS bit 0, so a keyword\nprinted next gets a space before it.","comment":"A space?"},
        "1372": {"synopsis":"Clears FLAGS bit 0 and prints A.","comment":"The last printed is not a space"},
        "1376": {"synopsis":"Writes the character in A into the display file. Works on the alternate registers (EXX at\nboth ends), the print registers: DE = the next display byte, C = the columns left + 1, B =\nthe lines left.\n- Bit 6 clear ($00-$3F, $80-$BF): one column. When the line is full, a $76 is written\n  first and the character starts the next line.\n- Bit 6 set (the $76): ends the line (C = 0); the next character starts a new line.\nStarting a line counts B down; when none is left, nothing is written. DISPLAY_ROOM checks\nthere is room below the stack.\nTokens are spelled out by PRINT_TOKEN before they get here.\nExit: carry set if written; carry clear and Z set if no line was left.","comment":"The print registers"},
        "1377": {"comment":"H = the character"},
        "1378": {"comment":"Two shifts left ..."},
        "1379": {"comment":"... carry = the character's bit 6"},
        "1380": {"comment":"One column used (S and Z: was C 0 or 1?)"},
        "1381": {"comment":"Bit 6 clear: a character"},
        "1383": {"comment":"The $76: C = 0, the line ends"},
        "1385": {"synopsis":"Decides by C (before the DEC): 0 = a new line starts here, 1 = the line is full.","comment":"C was 0: the previous line ended"},
        "1388": {"comment":"The $76: write it"},
        "1390": {"comment":"Room on this line"},
        "1392": {"comment":"Line full: end it with a $76"},
        "1396": {"synopsis":"A new line: C = 32 columns, one line fewer (B).","comment":"The $76 keeps C = 0"},
        "1398": {"comment":"C = $20: 32 columns, this character included"},
        "1400": {"synopsis":"Counts the line in B; none left: return with carry clear.","comment":"Carry clear: not written"},
        "1402": {"comment":"No line left"},
        "1404": {"synopsis":"Writes the character at DE if DISPLAY_ROOM allows.","comment":"L = the lines left"},
        "1405": {"comment":"Room below the stack? A = the character"},
        "1408": {"comment":"Write it"},
        "1410": {"synopsis":"Back to the main registers."},
        "1412": {"synopsis":"Spells out the token in A ($C0-$FF) from KEYWORD_TABLE (FIND_TOKEN). A word starting with\na letter or digit gets a space before it unless FLAGS bit 0 says the last printed was a\nspace; a word ending with one gets a space after it, which sets FLAGS bit 0.\nExit: carry set if all printed, clear if no room.","comment":"BC = the spelling; carry: it starts with a letter or digit"},
        "1415": {"comment":"A symbol: no space before"},
        "1417": {"comment":"A space just printed?"},
        "1423": {"comment":"A space before the word"},
        "1426": {"synopsis":"Prints the spelling's characters up to the one with bit 7 set.","comment":"The character"},
        "1433": {"comment":"Again, to test bit 7"},
        "1435": {"comment":"Bit 7 into the carry; A = the code x 2"},
        "1436": {"comment":"Not the last: on"},
        "1438": {"comment":"The last a symbol (below $1C)? No space after"},
        "1441": {"comment":"A space after, and FLAGS bit 0 set"},
        "1448": {"synopsis":"BC = the spelling of token A in KEYWORD_TABLE: entry A - $D4 + 1, found by counting the\nend marks (bit 7). A token below $D4 gets the first spelling, '?'.\nExit: BC = the spelling; A = its first character + $E4, carry set if that is a letter or\ndigit ($1C or above). HL kept.","comment":"HL = KEYWORD_TABLE"},
        "1452": {"comment":"A = the token - $D4"},
        "1454": {"comment":"Below $D4: the first spelling"},
        "1456": {"comment":"B = how many end marks to pass"},
        "1458": {"synopsis":"Passes one spelling.","comment":"Its last character?"},
        "1463": {"comment":"Next spelling"},
        "1465": {"synopsis":"BC = the spelling; tests its first character.","comment":"BC = HL"},
        "1471": {"comment":"Carry if $1C or above: a digit or letter"},
        "1474": {"synopsis":"Ends the current display line and writes a $76 for each line left (B), so the lines not\nused stay empty; DF_END = the byte after. When C = 0 (the line just ended with its $76) one\nfewer is needed.\nReturns with the print registers as the main set (one EXX, no second).","comment":"The print registers"},
        "1476": {"comment":"No line left?"},
        "1479": {"comment":"C = 0: the current line already ended"},
        "1484": {"synopsis":"Writes one $76."},
        "1486": {"synopsis":"Counts the lines in B."},
        "1488": {"synopsis":"DF_END = DE."},
        "1493": {"synopsis":"Opens BC bytes at HL: moves everything from HL up to DF_END up by BC, and adds BC to the\nsystem variables that point above HL (POINTERS).\nExit: DE = the last byte of the gap (HL + BC - 1), HL = HL - 1.","comment":"Move the pointers"},
        "1496": {"comment":"HL = the new DF_END"},
        "1500": {"comment":"Copy from the old end down to HL"},
        "1503": {"synopsis":"Adds BC to each of VARS, E_LINE, D_FILE, DF_EA and DF_END that is above HL.\nEntry: HL = the place, BC = the change (negative to reclaim).\nExit: BC = the bytes from HL to the old DF_END (inclusive), DE = the old DF_END,\nHL = the place. AF kept.","comment":"Keep AF"},
        "1505": {"comment":"HL = VARS's address, the first of the five"},
        "1508": {"comment":"Five pointers"},
        "1510": {"synopsis":"One pointer: moved if it is above the place.","comment":"DE = the pointer"},
        "1513": {"comment":"HL = the place"},
        "1515": {"comment":"Carry if the pointer is above it"},
        "1519": {"comment":"Not above: leave it"},
        "1522": {"comment":"DE = pointer + BC"},
        "1525": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "1529": {"comment":"DE = the old value"},
        "1530": {"synopsis":"The next pointer."},
        "1534": {"comment":"HL = the old DF_END"},
        "1535": {"comment":"DE = the place"},
        "1538": {"comment":"BC = old DF_END - place + 1"},
        "1546": {"synopsis":"Finds the first program line numbered HL or more. The program starts at $4028; a line\nis its number (high byte first), its text and a $76 (NEXT_ITEM).\nEntry: HL = the line number.\nExit: HL = that line, or the end of the program when there is none (anything in the\nvariables area starts with a byte of $40 or more, above any line number's high byte);\nDE = the line before it ($4028 when it is the first); BC = the number; Z set if the\nline has exactly that number.","comment":"BC = the number"},
        "1547": {"comment":"From the program's start"},
        "1552": {"synopsis":"One line: stop at the first numbered BC or more.","comment":"BC = the number again"},
        "1553": {"comment":"HL = this line, DE = the one before"},
        "1557": {"comment":"This one, or the end"},
        "1559": {"comment":"DE = the next line"},
        "1564": {"synopsis":"Compares the number of the line at HL (high byte first) with BC.\nExit: Z set if equal, carry set if the line's number is smaller. HL kept; A changed.","comment":"High bytes"},
        "1566": {"comment":"They differ: that decides"},
        "1572": {"synopsis":"Finds the item after the one at HL, a program line or a variable, by the first byte's\nbits 7-5:\n- 00x: a line: two bytes of number, the text, a $76.\n- 100: the first byte, then up to the next $01 (the '\"' character).\n- 101: the first byte, a byte n, then n + 1 words.\n- 010 / 110: the first byte and the following ones up to one with bit 7 set, then 2 / 6\n  bytes.\n- 011 / 111: the first byte alone, then 2 / 6 bytes.\nExit: HL = the item, DE = the next one, BC = the item's length.","comment":"Keep the item"},
        "1574": {"comment":"Carry = bit 7, sign = bit 6"},
        "1575": {"comment":"Bit 6 set"},
        "1578": {"comment":"Bit 7 set, bit 6 clear"},
        "1580": {"comment":"A line: past its number ..."},
        "1581": {"comment":"... to its $76"},
        "1583": {"synopsis":"Searches from HL + 1 for the byte in A ($76, or $01 for kind 100).","comment":"Past the first byte"},
        "1585": {"comment":"HL = just past it"},
        "1589": {"synopsis":"Bit 6 set: BC = 2 for bit 7 set (6 bytes follow), else 0 (2 bytes).","comment":"BC = 2"},
        "1594": {"comment":"Bit 7 clear: BC = 0"},
        "1595": {"synopsis":"Bit 5 set: the name is the first byte; clear: it runs on to a byte with bit 7 set.","comment":"Bit 6 out"},
        "1596": {"synopsis":"The next byte, until the bit tested is set.","comment":"Bit 5 first, then each byte's bit 7"},
        "1603": {"synopsis":"Bit 7 set, bit 6 clear: by bit 5, kind 100 (to the $01) or 101 (n + 1 words).","comment":"A = bit 5"},
        "1607": {"comment":"Kind 100: search for $01"},
        "1609": {"comment":"Kind 101: the byte n"},
        "1612": {"comment":"BC = n"},
        "1615": {"synopsis":"HL += 2 x (BC + 1).","comment":"BC + 1 words"},
        "1618": {"synopsis":"DE = the next item, HL = this one, BC = its length.","comment":"DE = the item"},
        "1619": {"synopsis":"BC = HL - DE, then HL and DE swap. Also called on its own (from $0663).","comment":"Clear carry"},
        "1627": {"synopsis":"Removes all the variables: VARS up to the byte before E_LINE's $80 end mark, which stays\n(RECLAIM_RANGE). CLEAR's routine, and RUN_CMD's tail.","comment":"HL = E_LINE - 1, the $80"},
        "1635": {"synopsis":"Removes the bytes from DE up to HL - 1 (RECLAIM).\nEntry: DE = the first byte, HL = the byte after the last.","comment":"BC = HL - DE; HL = the first byte"},
        "1638": {"synopsis":"Removes BC bytes at HL: subtracts BC from the system variables that point above HL\n(POINTERS) and moves everything above the bytes down to HL.\nExit: HL = HL.","comment":"Keep the count"},
        "1639": {"comment":"BC = -BC"},
        "1646": {"comment":"Move the pointers down"},
        "1649": {"comment":"HL = the old DF_END, DE = the place"},
        "1651": {"comment":"HL = the first byte kept"},
        "1653": {"comment":"Copy it all down"},
        "1657": {"synopsis":"Reads decimal digits from the character at HL on (NEXT_CHAR_RST), into HL' = the number\n(0 when there is none) and RESULT. Once the high byte is above $0D it is held at $0D before\neach x 10, so a long number stays below $8C00.\nExit: HL = CH_ADD, at the first character after the digits; carry = bit 15 of the number.\nChanges: A, and BC', DE'.","comment":"A = the character"},
        "1658": {"comment":"The number's registers"},
        "1659": {"comment":"HL = 0, B = 0"},
        "1663": {"synopsis":"A digit ($1C-$25)? Then HL = HL x 10 + it.","comment":"A = the digit, if it is one"},
        "1667": {"comment":"Above 9: not a digit"},
        "1672": {"comment":"Hold H at $0D at most"},
        "1678": {"synopsis":"HL = HL x 10 + C.","comment":"DE = HL"},
        "1682": {"comment":"x 5"},
        "1683": {"comment":"x 10"},
        "1684": {"comment":"+ the digit"},
        "1686": {"comment":"The next character"},
        "1690": {"synopsis":"Stores the number in RESULT; carry = its bit 15.","comment":"A = the high byte"},
        "1695": {"comment":"Carry = bit 15"},
        "1697": {"synopsis":"Prints BC as a signed number: '-' for a negative one, then its digits without leading\nzeros (PRINT_PLACE from 10000, then PRINT_DIGITS).\nExit: carry set if printed, clear if no room; DE and HL kept.","comment":"Keep DE and HL"},
        "1701": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "1705": {"comment":"'-'"},
        "1710": {"comment":"No room"},
        "1712": {"comment":"HL = -BC (the carry is set here)"},
        "1717": {"synopsis":"The digits of HL, no leading zeros (E = $FF).","comment":"E = $FF: no leading zeros"},
        "1719": {"comment":"Ten thousands"},
        "1727": {"synopsis":"Prints the line number at HL (high byte first) in four columns, leading zeros as spaces.\nExit: HL = the number's second byte; DE kept; carry set if printed, clear if no room.","comment":"Keep DE"},
        "1732": {"comment":"HL = the number"},
        "1733": {"comment":"E = $00: leading zeros print as spaces"},
        "1735": {"comment":"Carry: print every place"},
        "1736": {"synopsis":"Prints HL's thousands, hundreds, tens and units (PRINT_PLACE), each while the last was\nprinted (carry set). Also reached from PN_DIGITS.","comment":"Thousands"},
        "1742": {"comment":"Hundreds"},
        "1748": {"comment":"Tens"},
        "1753": {"comment":"Units"},
        "1757": {"synopsis":"PRINT_NUMBER's and PRINT_LINE_NO's end: HL and DE back."},
        "1760": {"synopsis":"Only while running (FLAGS bit 7 set): loads the print registers from DF_EA (the display\nposition) and S_POSN, and returns. Otherwise returns from its caller, so nothing is printed.","comment":"Running?"},
        "1764": {"comment":"HL = the return address"},
        "1765": {"comment":"Not running: return from the caller"},
        "1776": {"synopsis":"USR(n): jumps to n; the machine code returns its result in HL (FN_RESULT). Also\nPRINT_IF_RUN's return.","comment":"Go to HL"},
        "1777": {"synopsis":"Prints the expression just evaluated: a number (PRINT_NUMBER) or a string's text up to\nits '\"' (tokens in it spelled out), at the print position (PRINT_IF_RUN), and keeps the\nnew position (PRINT_SAVE_POS). Nothing after an error, or when checking. No room: error\ncode 4 (report 5).","comment":"An error recorded: nothing"},
        "1782": {"comment":"Checking: PRINT_IF_RUN returns from PRINT_RESULT"},
        "1785": {"comment":"HL = RESULT"},
        "1788": {"comment":"A number?"},
        "1796": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "1801": {"synopsis":"Prints a character of the string.","comment":"Print it"},
        "1802": {"synopsis":"No room: the screen is full.","comment":"No room"},
        "1804": {"synopsis":"The string's next byte: '\"' ends it, a token is spelled out.","comment":"The byte"},
        "1806": {"comment":"The '\"': done"},
        "1810": {"comment":"Bit 6 set: a token"},
        "1819": {"synopsis":"Ends the print line with a $76 (when running) and keeps the position.","comment":"Running? The print registers"},
        "1822": {"comment":"$76: the line ends"},
        "1827": {"synopsis":"Printed: keep the position; otherwise the screen is full.","comment":"Room: keep the position"},
        "1829": {"synopsis":"No room on the screen: error code 4 (report 5). Observed: a loop printing a line at a\ntime stopped with 5/10 after 23 lines."},
        "1831": {"synopsis":"The ',' separator: prints spaces up to the next column that is a multiple of 8\n(0, 8, 16, 24). Observed: PRINT 1,2,3 put the numbers 8 columns apart.","comment":"Running? The print registers"},
        "1834": {"comment":"No space needed before a keyword"},
        "1838": {"synopsis":"One space, until the columns left are a multiple of 8.","comment":"A space"},
        "1843": {"comment":"A = the columns left + 1"},
        "1850": {"synopsis":"Keeps the print position: S_POSN = the print registers' BC, DF_EA and DF_END = their DE\n(DISPLAY_ENDS).","comment":"The print registers"},
        "1851": {"comment":"HL = the display position"},
        "1852": {"synopsis":"S_POSN = BC; DF_EA and DF_END = HL. Returns.\nThe tail of CLS_DISPLAY, also reached from $073A.","comment":"S_POSN = BC"},
        "1856": {"comment":"DF_EA = HL"},
        "1859": {"comment":"DF_END = HL"},
        "1863": {"synopsis":"Empties the display file: a single $76 at D_FILE. DF_EA and DF_END become the byte\nafter it, and S_POSN becomes $1721 (DISPLAY_ENDS).\nChanges: HL, BC.","comment":"HL = D_FILE"},
        "1866": {"comment":"The display file is just this $76"},
        "1869": {"comment":"S_POSN's value: column 33 (low byte $21), line 23 (high byte $17)"},
        "1872": {"comment":"Store S_POSN, DF_EA and DF_END and return"},
        "1874": {"synopsis":"The syntax table. $0752-$076B: one offset per command token $E6-$FF; token T's entry is at\n$0752 + (T - $E6) + the offset (LINE_SCAN). From SYNTAX_ENTRIES, the entries, read item by\nitem by SYNTAX_ITEM: a byte with bit 7 set is a token that must come next; 0-6 is an operand\nclass (CLASS_TABLE); classes 0, 3 and 5 are followed by the command routine's address, and\nclass 2 ends an entry.\n$E6 LIST: 3 LIST_CMD / $E7 RETURN: 0 RETURN_CMD / $E8 CLS: 0 CLS_DISPLAY /\n$E9 DIM: 4 ( 6 ) 0 DIM_CMD / $EA SAVE: 0 $01B6 / $EB FOR: 4 = 6 TO 5 FOR_CMD /\n$EC GO TO: 6 0 GOTO_CMD / $ED POKE: 6 , 5 POKE_CMD / $EE INPUT: 1 0 INPUT_CMD /\n$EF RANDOMISE: 3 RANDOMISE_CMD / $F0 LET: 1 = 2 / $F3 NEXT: 4 0 NEXT_CMD / $F4 PRINT: 5 PRINT_CMD /\n$F6 NEW: 0 START / $F7 RUN: 3 RUN_CMD / $F8 STOP: 0 STOP_CMD / $F9 CONTINUE: 0 CONTINUE_CMD /\n$FA IF: 6 THEN 5 IF_CMD / $FB GO SUB: 6 0 GOSUB_CMD / $FC LOAD: 0 $0206 / $FD CLEAR: 0 CLEAR_VARS /\n$FE REM: 5 SKIP_TO_END / $F1, $F2, $F5, $FF ('?'): 5 NO_STATEMENT (one shared entry, $07BB).\nNEW's entry is not in the table's bytes: its offset points at $07C3, inside LINE_SCAN, where\nthe bytes $00 $00 $00 of LD HL,$0000 and NOP read as class 0 and the address $0000."},
        "1900": {"synopsis":"The syntax entries, LET's ($076C) first; SYNTAX_TABLE lists every one."},
        "1982": {"synopsis":"Checks (FLAGS bit 7 clear, EDIT_LINE_SHOW) or runs (bit 7 set, RUN_STATEMENT) the line at\nHL. A line holds one statement; IF's THEN goes on with another (LS_STATEMENT).\n1. CH_ADD = HL - 1, X_PTR = 0.\n2. With FLAGX bit 5 set the line is an INPUT answer: an expression of the type FLAGX bit 6\n   asks for (ASSIGN_CHECK).\n3. Otherwise a line number may come first (READ_NUMBER); 10000 or more is an error.\n   FLAGX bit 7 is set up to the first token, so a cursor there shows K.\n4. An empty line returns. The first character must be a command token ($E6-$FF), whose\n   entry in SYNTAX_TABLE is followed item by item (SYNTAX_ITEM); the entry ends by\n   running the command routine (when running) or by checking the line ends.\nErrors: MARK_ERROR keeps the first one's place in X_PTR; a line that does not start with a\ncommand is passed over to its $76 (LS_ERROR). In runs, X_PTR marks the place an error was\nfound (10000 PRINT 1: just after the number).","comment":"CH_ADD = HL - 1"},
        "1986": {"comment":"$07C3-$07C5 ($00 $00 $00) are also NEW's syntax entry"},
        "1989": {"comment":"The entry's last byte (see SYNTAX_TABLE)"},
        "1990": {"comment":"X_PTR = 0: no error marker"},
        "1993": {"comment":"FLAGX"},
        "1996": {"comment":"Bit 5: an INPUT answer?"},
        "2000": {"comment":"Yes: no keyword expected"},
        "2002": {"comment":"B = FLAGX: bit 6 is the answer's type"},
        "2003": {"comment":"The first character"},
        "2004": {"comment":"Check, or assign, the answer"},
        "2007": {"synopsis":"A statement line: an optional line number, then the statement.","comment":"FLAGX bit 7: a keyword is expected"},
        "2009": {"comment":"The first character"},
        "2010": {"comment":"A line number, if one is there"},
        "2013": {"comment":"32768 or more: an error"},
        "2016": {"comment":"Carry if the number is 10000 or more"},
        "2021": {"synopsis":"Marks an error (when carry is set), then tries the next character as a statement start.","comment":"An error here"},
        "2024": {"synopsis":"The statement: a command token and its operands. Also entered by IF ($08C1) for the\nstatement after THEN.","comment":"The first character"},
        "2027": {"comment":"Past the keyword's place: the cursor shows L from here"},
        "2031": {"comment":"RESULT = 0: what a command gets without an operand"},
        "2038": {"comment":"The line's end: done"},
        "2041": {"comment":"C = the token"},
        "2042": {"comment":"Step past it"},
        "2044": {"comment":"Not a command token ($E6-$FF): an error"},
        "2048": {"comment":"BC = the token - $E6"},
        "2049": {"comment":"HL = its offset's address in SYNTAX_TABLE"},
        "2054": {"comment":"Add the offset: HL = the token's entry"},
        "2057": {"synopsis":"The next item of the entry: SYNTAX_ITEM returns here after each one.","comment":"HL = T_ADDR, the next item"},
        "2060": {"synopsis":"One item of a syntax entry, at HL; T_ADDR = the item after it.\n- Bit 7 set: the token that must come next (SYNTAX_TOKEN).\n- 0-6: an operand class; its routine (CLASS_TABLE) is entered with A = the current\n  character and SYNTAX_NEXT as its return.","comment":"The item"},
        "2062": {"comment":"T_ADDR: the item after it"},
        "2065": {"comment":"Return to SYNTAX_NEXT for the next item"},
        "2070": {"comment":"Bit 7: a token"},
        "2073": {"comment":"A class: HL = its offset's address"},
        "2079": {"comment":"+ the offset: the class routine"},
        "2081": {"comment":"Its address on the stack ..."},
        "2082": {"comment":"... A = the current character ..."},
        "2085": {"comment":"... and go there"},
        "2086": {"synopsis":"The current character must be the token in C; steps past it. Meeting THEN ($D5) sets\nFLAGX bit 7: a keyword follows, so a cursor there shows K.","comment":"The current character"},
        "2089": {"comment":"THEN?"},
        "2097": {"synopsis":"Compares; a different character is an error.","comment":"The token asked for?"},
        "2098": {"comment":"No: mark the error"},
        "2100": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "2102": {"synopsis":"The operand classes' routines, one offset byte per class 0-6: class n's routine is at\n$0836 + n + the byte (SYNTAX_ITEM). CLASS_0 $0855, CLASS_1 $086A, CLASS_2 $0885,\nCLASS_3 $0850, CLASS_4 $089E, CLASS_5 $0856, CLASS_6 $08A8."},
        "2109": {"synopsis":"Running: returns. Checking: the statement must end here. Marks an error unless HL is at\nthe $76, passes over the rest of the line, and returns from LINE_SCAN (its caller was\nentered from the syntax entry, with SYNTAX_NEXT already dropped).","comment":"Running?"},
        "2114": {"comment":"Checking: drop the return, so the RET below leaves LINE_SCAN"},
        "2115": {"synopsis":"The statement must end at HL.","comment":"The character at HL"},
        "2116": {"synopsis":"The routine for the tokens $F1, $F2, $F5 and $FF (spelt '?'): an error unless the line\nends at once; then passes over the rest of the line.","comment":"At the $76?"},
        "2118": {"comment":"No: mark the error"},
        "2121": {"comment":"The character"},
        "2122": {"synopsis":"Passes over characters up to the line's $76 and returns. REM's routine: a REM line is\nnever run.","comment":"The end?"},
        "2125": {"comment":"The next character"},
        "2128": {"synopsis":"Class 3: an optional number (CLASS_6) before the end, then as CLASS_0. Without one the\ncommand gets RESULT = 0. LIST, RANDOMISE, RUN.","comment":"The end at once?"},
        "2130": {"comment":"No: a numeric expression"},
        "2133": {"synopsis":"Class 0: the statement ends here (CHECK_END); when running, the command routine whose\naddress follows the class byte runs (JUMP_ROUTINE).","comment":"Z set: CHECK_END below"},
        "2134": {"synopsis":"Class 5: the command routine runs at once, checking as well as running: it reads its own\noperands and tests FLAGS bit 7 itself. PRINT, the tails of FOR, POKE and IF, REM.\n(Entered with Z clear, so the CALL Z is passed.)","comment":"Drop SYNTAX_NEXT: the entry ends"},
        "2135": {"comment":"Class 0 only: the end, or out of LINE_SCAN"},
        "2138": {"comment":"Keep HL"},
        "2139": {"comment":"The routine's address, after the class byte"},
        "2146": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the routine BC with BC = RESULT (0 unless an operand set it) and A = B | C,\nso Z means 0. The routine's RET leaves LINE_SCAN.","comment":"The routine as the return address"},
        "2153": {"comment":"Go there"},
        "2154": {"synopsis":"Class 1: a variable to assign to (LET, INPUT). It must start with a letter (IS_LETTER).\nThe name is scanned by $0AAD (read later) with FLAGS bit 7 clear; CLASS_2 then compares\nFLAGS bit 6 from that scan with the expression's type (observed: LET A=\"X\" is marked as\nan error). When running, DEST = the name's place in the line.","comment":"A letter?"},
        "2157": {"comment":"No: an error"},
        "2159": {"comment":"Checking: just scan the name"},
        "2166": {"comment":"Running: DEST = the name's place"},
        "2169": {"comment":"Scan it as when checking"},
        "2181": {"synopsis":"Class 2: the expression assigned (LET's = ...), the entry's last item. B = FLAGS, whose\nbit 6 holds the variable's type (CLASS_1).","comment":"Drop SYNTAX_NEXT"},
        "2185": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the expression (EXPR_RST).\n- Running: jumps to $0C3D (the assignment, read later) with BC = RESULT.\n- Checking: its type (FLAGS bit 6) must be the one bit 6 of B asks for, and the line\n  must end after it.\nAlso LINE_SCAN's entry for an INPUT answer, with B = FLAGX.","comment":"Keep the type asked for"},
        "2186": {"comment":"Evaluate"},
        "2187": {"comment":"D = the type asked for"},
        "2188": {"comment":"The assignment routine"},
        "2194": {"comment":"Running: assign"},
        "2198": {"comment":"Checking: do the types differ?"},
        "2201": {"comment":"Yes: an error"},
        "2204": {"comment":"The line must end here"},
        "2206": {"synopsis":"Class 4: a single letter (FOR, NEXT, DIM); DEST = its place. Steps past it.","comment":"DEST = the letter's place"},
        "2209": {"comment":"A letter?"},
        "2212": {"comment":"No: an error"},
        "2214": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "2216": {"synopsis":"Class 6: a numeric expression (EXPR_RST). A string is an error (MARK_ERROR).\nAlso called by CLASS_3 and by command routines.","comment":"Evaluate"},
        "2217": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 6: a number?"},
        "2222": {"synopsis":"Records a syntax error at HL: X_PTR = HL, unless X_PTR already marks one (the first is\nkept). LIST_TEXT shows it as an inverse S; KEY_NEWLINE will not take the line.","comment":"A = X_PTR's low | high byte"},
        "2228": {"comment":"Already marked"},
        "2233": {"synopsis":"IF condition THEN statement (class 5 after the condition): A = B | C, the condition.\nTrue (not 0): the statement after THEN runs (LS_STATEMENT). False: the rest of the line is\npassed over when running; when checking it is checked anyway.\nObserved: IF 1=1 THEN PRINT \"T\" printed, IF 1=2 THEN PRINT \"F\" did not.","comment":"True?"},
        "2235": {"comment":"False and running: pass over the rest"},
        "2241": {"synopsis":"The statement after THEN.","comment":"Scan, or run, it"},
        "2244": {"synopsis":"FOR v = first TO limit (class 5): BC = first. Evaluates the limit (CLASS_6), then when\nrunning assigns first to v (ASSIGN) and turns v into a FOR variable: its kind byte gets bit\n7 (kind 111) and 4 bytes follow the value, made the first time (MAKE_ROOM): the limit and\nPPC + 1, the line NEXT goes back to. There is no STEP: NEXT adds 1.\nObserved: FOR I=1 TO 3 in line 10 made EE 04 00 03 00 0B 00 by the loop's end.","comment":"Keep first"},
        "2245": {"comment":"The limit"},
        "2249": {"comment":"The end of the statement (checking leaves here)"},
        "2252": {"comment":"Keep the limit"},
        "2256": {"comment":"v = first; HL = the value's low byte"},
        "2260": {"comment":"An error: done"},
        "2266": {"comment":"The kind byte"},
        "2267": {"comment":"A FOR variable already? ..."},
        "2269": {"comment":"... it is one now"},
        "2273": {"comment":"Already: the 4 bytes are there"},
        "2275": {"comment":"Make room for the limit and the line"},
        "2282": {"synopsis":"Stores the limit and PPC + 1 after the value.","comment":"The 4 bytes"},
        "2284": {"comment":"The limit"},
        "2288": {"comment":"The line after the FOR's: PPC + 1"},
        "2297": {"synopsis":"NEXT v (class 4, then 0): v + 1; while it is not above the limit, goes to the line\nstored with it (GOTO_CMD). v must exist (report 2) and be a FOR variable (report 1).","comment":"Find v"},
        "2303": {"comment":"Not found: done (report 2)"},
        "2308": {"comment":"HL = the value's high byte"},
        "2311": {"comment":"Kind bit 7: a FOR variable?"},
        "2315": {"comment":"v + 1 ..."},
        "2317": {"comment":"... stored"},
        "2321": {"comment":"BC = the limit"},
        "2325": {"comment":"HL = the limit, DE = v"},
        "2326": {"comment":"Is the limit below v?"},
        "2330": {"comment":"Yes: the loop is done"},
        "2332": {"comment":"BC = the stored line"},
        "2335": {"comment":"Go there"},
        "2337": {"synopsis":"Not a FOR variable: error code 0 (report 1)."},
        "2339": {"synopsis":"RANDOMISE [n]: SEED = n, or FRAMES when n is 0 or missing.","comment":"Z: n is 0"},
        "2341": {"comment":"BC = FRAMES"},
        "2345": {"synopsis":"SEED = BC (n, or FRAMES).","comment":"SEED = BC"},
        "2350": {"synopsis":"STOP: error code 8, report 9. RUN_STOP then makes OLDPPC = PPC + 1, where CONTINUE\ngoes on (observed: STOP in line 30 gave 9/30, OLDPPC = 31)."},
        "2352": {"synopsis":"CONTINUE: goes to line OLDPPC (GOTO_CMD).","comment":"BC = OLDPPC"},
        "2356": {"synopsis":"GO TO n: PPC = BC and FLAGS bit 3 set, so RUN_STATEMENT runs line n (or the next one\nafter it) next. Also CONTINUE's, RUN's and other routines' way to jump.","comment":"PPC = the line"},
        "2360": {"comment":"FLAGS bit 3: go to line PPC"},
        "2365": {"synopsis":"RUN [n]: goes to line n (GOTO_CMD; 0 without n: the first line) and removes the variables\n(CLEAR_VARS).","comment":"PPC = n"},
        "2368": {"comment":"Clear the variables"},
        "2371": {"synopsis":"GO SUB n: the line to return to, PPC + 1, goes on the machine stack under the routine's\nreturn address, so it stays there when the statement ends; then GOTO_CMD, and TEST_ROOM\nchecks 6 bytes more fit below the stack.\nObserved: 10 GO SUB 100 / 20 PRINT \"E\" / 30 STOP / 100 PRINT \"S\"; / 110 RETURN printed SE, 9/30.","comment":"HL = PPC + 1"},
        "2375": {"comment":"Under the return address"},
        "2376": {"comment":"The return address back on top"},
        "2377": {"comment":"PPC = n"},
        "2380": {"comment":"6 bytes"},
        "2383": {"synopsis":"Checks there is room for BC more bytes: DISPLAY_ROOM with DF_END + BC, the line byte of\nS_POSN, and A kept. Returns with carry set if there is; otherwise records error code $03\n(report 4) and returns with carry clear.","comment":"HL = DF_END + BC"},
        "2388": {"comment":"L = S_POSN's line byte"},
        "2391": {"comment":"H = A, which DISPLAY_ROOM returns"},
        "2392": {"synopsis":"Checks the display file can grow: returns when DE + L + $13 is below SP, else records error\ncode $03 (report 4) through ERROR_RST and returns with carry clear.\nEntry: DE = the display byte, L = the lines left, H = the character.\nExit: A = the character, carry set.","comment":"$13 + the lines left"},
        "2399": {"comment":"HL = DE + L + $13"},
        "2400": {"comment":"Below the stack: room"},
        "2405": {"synopsis":"RETURN: takes the word under the return address. A GO SUB's line goes to GOTO_CMD; the\nword start-up left at the stack's bottom (A = $3F at RAM_FILL, a high byte no line number\nhas) means there was no GO SUB: it is put back and error code 6 (report 7) recorded.\nObserved: RETURN as a command gave 7/-2.","comment":"The return address"},
        "2406": {"comment":"The word under it"},
        "2408": {"comment":"High byte $3F: the stack's bottom"},
        "2411": {"comment":"A GO SUB's line: go there"},
        "2413": {"comment":"Put the bottom word back"},
        "2418": {"synopsis":"PRINT: items separated by ';' (nothing between) or ',' (PRINT_COMMA), each an expression\n(EXPR_RST, PRINT_RESULT). The line ends with a $76 (PRINT_NEWLINE) unless the statement\nends with a separator. Checks as well as runs (class 5).\nObserved: PRINT \"A\";\"B\" AB; PRINT \"X\"; leaves the line open.","comment":"At the end?"},
        "2421": {"comment":"PRINT alone: a new line"},
        "2424": {"synopsis":"A separator, or an item.","comment":"',' ($D8) or ';' ($D7) gives 0 ..."},
        "2426": {"comment":"... carry set for ';'"},
        "2430": {"comment":"An item: evaluate ..."},
        "2431": {"comment":"... and print it"},
        "2434": {"comment":"What follows it"},
        "2441": {"comment":"A separator"},
        "2443": {"comment":"Otherwise the statement must end ..."},
        "2446": {"comment":"... with a new line"},
        "2449": {"synopsis":"After a separator: ',' tabs; the end of the line after one leaves the print line open.","comment":"',' only"},
        "2452": {"comment":"The next character"},
        "2455": {"comment":"The end: no new line"},
        "2458": {"synopsis":"INPUT v (class 1, then 0; a program line only, report 8 as a command): ends the run here\n(drops LINE_SCAN's return), sets FLAGX bit 5 (an answer is awaited) and bit 6 = v's type,\nand gives the editor an answer line in the workspace: the cursor and $76, or for a string\n'\"', the cursor, '\"', $76. The lower part of the screen takes the lines left after the output\n(DF_SZ = S_POSN's line byte + 1); then EDIT_LINE_SHOW. KEY_NEWLINE runs the answer.\nObserved: INPUT A$ waited with 01 B0 01 76, FLAGX $20; the answer HI was printed by line 20.","comment":"PPC's high byte: bit 7 set for a command"},
        "2462": {"comment":"A command: report 8"},
        "2464": {"comment":"Drop LINE_SCAN's return: the run stops here"},
        "2468": {"comment":"An answer awaited"},
        "2472": {"comment":"v's type, from CLASS_1's scan"},
        "2477": {"comment":"A number's answer line: 2 bytes"},
        "2482": {"comment":"A string's: 4"},
        "2484": {"synopsis":"FLAGX bit 6 = the type; the answer line in the workspace.","comment":"FLAGX bit 6 = the type"},
        "2486": {"comment":"Room at the workspace's end"},
        "2488": {"comment":"The line's $76"},
        "2490": {"comment":"2 or 4, rotated: carry for 2"},
        "2495": {"comment":"A string: '\"' first ..."},
        "2497": {"comment":"... and before the $76"},
        "2498": {"synopsis":"The cursor; DF_SZ; then the editor.","comment":"The cursor"},
        "2501": {"comment":"The lines left, + 1"},
        "2508": {"comment":"Edit the answer"},
        "2511": {"synopsis":"INPUT as a command: error code 7 (report 8)."},
        "2513": {"synopsis":"POKE address, value (class 5 after the address): writes the value's low byte, when\nrunning and no error is recorded.","comment":"Keep the address"},
        "2514": {"comment":"The value"},
        "2516": {"comment":"The end (checking leaves here)"},
        "2519": {"comment":"The low byte"},
        "2527": {"comment":"Write it"},
        "2529": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression with 16-bit integers (from EXPR_RST, B = 0).\nOperands (EV_OPERAND): a number, a variable or function (VAR_SCAN), a string in quotes\n(RESULT = its text's address), a bracketed expression (BRACKET_EXPR), and the prefixes\n- (worked as 0 - x, priority 9) and NOT (as -1 - x, priority 4).\nOperators: - + * / AND OR ** = > <, with the priorities in PRIORITIES; strings take only\n= > < (their codes + 3, the string comparisons).\nThe machine stack holds the waiting operations: the left operand and a word of priority\n(high byte) and operator (low byte), over a bottom word of priority 0. Each new operator\nfirst carries out the waiting ones of the same or higher priority (EV_STACK), so equal\npriorities work left to right. The routines are in OP_TABLE; every result is a number\n(true -1, false 0 in runs).\nChecking (FLAGS bit 7 clear): nothing is computed; each operation's operand types are\nchecked instead.\nExit: RESULT, FLAGS bit 6 = the type.","comment":"C = 0"},
        "2530": {"comment":"The stack's bottom: priority 0"},
        "2531": {"synopsis":"An operand, by its first character.","comment":"A digit or a letter?"},
        "2536": {"comment":"B = 9, C = 0 (the - routine): the prefix -"},
        "2539": {"comment":"DE = 0: the left operand of 0 - x"},
        "2541": {"comment":"The - token?"},
        "2545": {"comment":"DE = -1, for NOT"},
        "2546": {"comment":"B = 4: NOT's priority"},
        "2548": {"comment":"NOT ($DB)?"},
        "2551": {"comment":"'(' ($DA)?"},
        "2554": {"comment":"The '\"' ($01)?"},
        "2558": {"comment":"A string: FLAGS bit 6 clear"},
        "2562": {"comment":"RESULT = the text's address, after the '\"'"},
        "2566": {"synopsis":"Walks the string to its closing '\"'; a $76 first is an error.","comment":"Next character"},
        "2567": {"comment":"The '\"' ($01): the end"},
        "2570": {"comment":"The $76 (A is one less): no closing '\"'"},
        "2574": {"synopsis":"Not an operand: an error, and the expression ends (B = 0).","comment":"Mark the error"},
        "2578": {"comment":"B = 0: end"},
        "2583": {"synopsis":"Stacks a prefix as an operation with its left operand DE: 0 for -, -1 for NOT.","comment":"The left operand"},
        "2584": {"comment":"Priority and routine"},
        "2585": {"synopsis":"Steps on to the next operand.","comment":"The next character"},
        "2588": {"synopsis":"A bracketed expression.","comment":"Evaluate it; past the ')'"},
        "2593": {"synopsis":"Steps past the closing '\"'.","comment":"Past the '\"'"},
        "2596": {"synopsis":"A letter: a variable or function (VAR_SCAN); a digit: a number.","comment":"A letter?"},
        "2605": {"synopsis":"A number (READ_NUMBER, into RESULT): 32768 or more is an error.","comment":"Read it"},
        "2611": {"comment":"A number: FLAGS bit 6 set"},
        "2615": {"synopsis":"After an operand: B = the operator's priority and C its code ($DC-$E5 less $DC); B = 0\nfor anything else, which ends the expression.","comment":"The next character"},
        "2619": {"comment":"B = 0: no operator"},
        "2622": {"comment":"Below $DC: not an operator"},
        "2626": {"comment":"Above $E5: not an operator"},
        "2630": {"comment":"C = the operator's code, 0-9"},
        "2631": {"comment":"B = its priority, from PRIORITIES"},
        "2636": {"synopsis":"Compares the new operator (B) with the waiting one on the stack (D). A higher new\npriority is stacked (EV_PUSH); otherwise the waiting one is done and the next compared.\nThe bottom word (priority 0) meeting the end (B = 0) returns.","comment":"D, E = the waiting priority and routine"},
        "2639": {"comment":"The new one binds tighter: stack it"},
        "2641": {"comment":"Z: the bottom word, so the expression is done"},
        "2645": {"comment":"Running?"},
        "2653": {"comment":"DE = the routine, from OP_TABLE"},
        "2661": {"comment":"Its return: EV_RESULT"},
        "2664": {"comment":"HL = the left operand"},
        "2666": {"comment":"DE = the right operand"},
        "2670": {"comment":"Run the routine: HL op DE"},
        "2671": {"synopsis":"Checking: the right operand's type must be a number for codes 0-9 and a string for\nthe string comparisons (10-12).","comment":"Code 10 and up: no carry"},
        "2674": {"comment":"A bit 6: the type the operator needs"},
        "2676": {"comment":"Compare with the operand's (FLAGS bit 6)"},
        "2686": {"comment":"Drop the left operand"},
        "2687": {"synopsis":"An operation's result: RESULT = HL, a number.","comment":"RESULT = HL"},
        "2690": {"comment":"Every result is a number"},
        "2696": {"synopsis":"Stacks the new operation over the waiting one: a string's operator becomes code + 3, and\nonly = > < (10-12) are allowed on strings.","comment":"The waiting one back"},
        "2698": {"comment":"The left operand a number?"},
        "2704": {"comment":"A string: code + 3"},
        "2707": {"comment":"Below 10: not a comparison, an error"},
        "2714": {"synopsis":"Stacks the left operand (RESULT) and the operator, then reads the next operand.","comment":"The left operand"},
        "2718": {"comment":"Priority and routine"},
        "2723": {"synopsis":"The operators' priorities, by code (token - $DC): - 6, + 6, * 8, / 7, AND 3, OR 2, ** 10,\n= 5, > 5, < 5. A higher number binds tighter; 0 is the stack's bottom, and the prefixes\nare stacked with 9 (-) and 4 (NOT)."},
        "2733": {"synopsis":"Scans the name at CH_ADD (a letter) and, when running, gets its value into RESULT:\n- A letter and '$': a string variable (FLAGS bit 6 clear).\n- A letter and '(': an array element; the subscript is evaluated (BRACKET_EXPR).\n- A longer name: a number variable; followed by '(' or '$(' it is a function, looked up\n  in FUNCTION_TABLE (VS_FUNCTION).\nFLAGS bit 6 = the type (1 a number), bit 5 set for a function.\nRunning: a function's routine runs; a variable is found by VAR_FIND.\nErrors: an unknown function, or an argument of the wrong type (MARK_ERROR).","comment":"Keep the name's address"},
        "2737": {"comment":"Not a function yet"},
        "2739": {"comment":"A number unless a '$' follows"},
        "2741": {"comment":"The character after the first letter"},
        "2742": {"comment":"'$': a string variable"},
        "2747": {"comment":"'(': an array"},
        "2752": {"synopsis":"Passes the name's other letters and digits.","comment":"A letter or digit?"},
        "2760": {"synopsis":"After a long name: '(' or '$(' makes it a function; anything else ends a number variable's\nname.","comment":"'('?"},
        "2764": {"comment":"Not '$' either: a variable"},
        "2770": {"comment":"'$' must be followed by '('"},
        "2774": {"synopsis":"Matches the name against FUNCTION_TABLE, entry by entry.","comment":"DE = one before the table"},
        "2777": {"synopsis":"An entry: back to the name's start.","comment":"HL = the name"},
        "2779": {"synopsis":"Compares the name's characters with the entry's.","comment":"C = the name's character"},
        "2780": {"comment":"Step on (CH_ADD too)"},
        "2785": {"comment":"The same: go on"},
        "2788": {"comment":"The entry's last character (bit 7, and bit 6 the argument's type)?"},
        "2793": {"comment":"And the name ends there, at '('?"},
        "2798": {"synopsis":"Not this entry: passes over it (its characters and routine word); a 0 ends the table.","comment":"The end of the table?"},
        "2800": {"comment":"No function by that name"},
        "2806": {"comment":"Past the routine word's first byte"},
        "2809": {"synopsis":"Found: evaluates the argument and checks its type. A name ending in '$' gives a string,\nany other a number.","comment":"Keep the entry"},
        "2810": {"comment":"The argument, in brackets"},
        "2814": {"comment":"HL = the name; the place after ')' kept"},
        "2818": {"comment":"The argument's type against the entry's bit 6"},
        "2822": {"comment":"Different: an error"},
        "2824": {"comment":"A function; a number ..."},
        "2831": {"comment":"... unless the name ends in '$'"},
        "2837": {"synopsis":"Checking: done. Running: the function's routine runs on the argument.","comment":"Running?"},
        "2839": {"comment":"Checking: done"},
        "2841": {"comment":"The routine returns to FN_RESULT"},
        "2846": {"comment":"DE = the routine, after the name"},
        "2851": {"comment":"HL = the argument"},
        "2854": {"comment":"Run the routine"},
        "2855": {"synopsis":"A bad name: marks an error.","comment":"Drop the name's address"},
        "2859": {"synopsis":"An array element: evaluates the subscript.","comment":"The subscript, in brackets"},
        "2864": {"synopsis":"A string variable: FLAGS bit 6 clear; past the '$'.","comment":"A string"},
        "2869": {"synopsis":"Checking: done. Running: finds the variable (VAR_FIND).","comment":"HL = the name"},
        "2875": {"synopsis":"Finds the variable named at HL in the line (running), from VARS on. The kind byte looked\nfor is the first letter with bits 7-5 set by the name: 000 a string (A$), 001 an array\n(A(n)), 010 a long name, 011 one letter. Bit 7 of the stored byte is ignored, so a FOR\nvariable is found as a number. A long name is compared to its end.\n- Number: RESULT = HL = its value; DE = the address of its high byte.\n- String: RESULT = HL = its text's address.\n- Array element n: 0 to the array's size byte, else error code 2 (report 3). The subscript\n  is evaluated here (CH_ADD kept); an error in it also becomes code 2.\nNot found: error code 1 (report 2), VAR_NOT_FOUND.\nAlso ASSIGN's lookup, with HL = DEST.","comment":"C = the first letter"},
        "2877": {"comment":"The character after it"},
        "2879": {"comment":"'('?"},
        "2884": {"comment":"Keep CH_ADD"},
        "2889": {"comment":"Evaluate the subscript"},
        "2893": {"comment":"CH_ADD back"},
        "2900": {"comment":"An error in the subscript?"},
        "2904": {"comment":"Then error code 2"},
        "2908": {"synopsis":"Not an array: the kind bits for a string, a long name or one letter.","comment":"Bit 5 off: a string's 000 ..."},
        "2910": {"comment":"'$': a string"},
        "2914": {"comment":"... a number: 010 ..."},
        "2916": {"comment":"A letter or digit next: a long name"},
        "2921": {"comment":"... one letter: 011"},
        "2923": {"synopsis":"Walks the variables area from VARS.","comment":"HL = VARS"},
        "2926": {"synopsis":"One item: its kind byte against C, bit 7 left out.","comment":"Bit 7 out"},
        "2929": {"comment":"$80: the end, not found"},
        "2933": {"comment":"Another variable"},
        "2935": {"comment":"Bit 6 into the carry, bit 5 into the sign"},
        "2937": {"comment":"Bit 5 set: one letter, or an array"},
        "2940": {"comment":"Both clear: a string"},
        "2942": {"comment":"A long name: compare the rest"},
        "2945": {"synopsis":"Compares the long name; its stored last character has bit 7 set.","comment":"The next characters"},
        "2951": {"comment":"The last character?"},
        "2956": {"comment":"And the name in the line ends there?"},
        "2962": {"synopsis":"Not this one.","comment":"The variable's address"},
        "2963": {"synopsis":"The next item (NEXT_ITEM).","comment":"Keep the kind"},
        "2971": {"synopsis":"A long name found: its value.","comment":"Drop the name"},
        "2972": {"synopsis":"A number found: its value.","comment":"Drop"},
        "2973": {"synopsis":"Reads the value word after HL: RESULT = HL = it, DE = the high byte's address.","comment":"The value"},
        "2980": {"synopsis":"Bit 6 set too: one letter (VF_VALUE). Otherwise an array: element RESULT, from 0 up to\nthe size byte.","comment":"One letter"},
        "2982": {"comment":"HL = the name; the array's address kept"},
        "2983": {"comment":"HL = the subscript"},
        "2986": {"comment":"H must be 0"},
        "2991": {"comment":"The size byte"},
        "2993": {"comment":"Above it: an error"},
        "2996": {"comment":"HL = the element's word, less one"},
        "3000": {"synopsis":"A string: RESULT = HL = its text's address.","comment":"Drop the name"},
        "3002": {"synopsis":"RESULT = HL. A function routine's return (VS_FN_FOUND), and VAR_FIND's end.","comment":"RESULT = HL"},
        "3006": {"synopsis":"A subscript out of range: error code 2 (report 3). The code byte, $0BBF, is also\nFUNCTION_TABLE's base (VS_FUNCTION starts one before the table)."},
        "3008": {"synopsis":"The functions: each name, its last character with bit 7 set and bit 6 set for a number\nargument, then the routine's address; a 0 ends the table ($0BEC). A routine is entered\nwith HL = the argument (a number, or a string's text address) and returns the result\nin HL (FN_RESULT).\nPEEK (number) PEEK_FN / CHR$ (number) CHR_FN / CODE (string) PEEK_FN /\nRND (number) RND_FN / TL$ (string) TL_FN / USR (number) USR_FN / STR$ (number) STR_FN /\nABS (number) ABS_HL.\nObserved: CODE(\"A\") 38, CHR$(38) \"A\", TL$(\"ABC\") \"BC\", STR$(-12) \"-12\", PEEK(0) 33, ABS(-3) 3."},
        "3053": {"synopsis":"RND(n): steps SEED to SEED x 77 mod 65537 (the 32-bit product's low word less its high\nword, + 1 on a borrow; a SEED of 0 becomes $FFB3), then returns 1 + (SEED x n) / 65536:\n1 to n.\nObserved after RANDOMISE 1: RND(10) 1, 1, 10, with SEED $004D, $1729, $F74F.","comment":"Keep n"},
        "3054": {"comment":"HL = SEED"},
        "3057": {"comment":"The multiplier, 77"},
        "3062": {"comment":"SEED 0"},
        "3064": {"comment":"BC:HL = SEED x 77"},
        "3068": {"comment":"Low word - high word: mod 65537"},
        "3072": {"comment":"A borrow: + 1"},
        "3075": {"synopsis":"SEED 0: becomes 0 - 77 = $FFB3."},
        "3077": {"synopsis":"The new SEED, then the result 1 + (SEED x n) / 65536.","comment":"SEED = HL"},
        "3080": {"comment":"DE = n"},
        "3081": {"comment":"BC = the high word of SEED x n"},
        "3086": {"comment":"+ 1"},
        "3088": {"synopsis":"STR$(n): prints n (PRINT_NUMBER) into 7 bytes of workspace, ended by '\"', and returns\ntheir address; without room, the empty string (EMPTY_STRING).","comment":"The print registers"},
        "3089": {"comment":"7 bytes: a sign, five digits and the '\"'"},
        "3092": {"comment":"The workspace bytes; DE = the first"},
        "3095": {"comment":"The string's address"},
        "3097": {"comment":"BC = n"},
        "3099": {"comment":"Print it into the workspace"},
        "3103": {"comment":"The closing '\"'"},
        "3106": {"synopsis":"Drops the stacked word and returns.","comment":"Drop"},
        "3108": {"synopsis":"PEEK(n): the byte at n. Also CODE(s): the code of the string's first character.","comment":"HL = the byte"},
        "3112": {"synopsis":"CHR$(n): a one-character string in two bytes of workspace: n's low byte and '\"'.","comment":"Two bytes"},
        "3119": {"comment":"The '\"' last ..."},
        "3122": {"comment":"... and the character before it: HL = the string"},
        "3124": {"synopsis":"No room: HL = $0C30, a ROM byte $01, the empty string \"\".","comment":"$0C30 holds $01"},
        "3128": {"synopsis":"TL$(s): s without its first character; the empty string stays empty.","comment":"The first character"},
        "3130": {"comment":"'\"' ($01): empty, keep it"},
        "3133": {"synopsis":"Stores BC (RESULT: a number, or a string's text address) in the variable named at DEST\n(LET; also FOR and INPUT). Nothing when an error is already recorded, or the subscript was\nbad (code 2).\nThe variable is looked up (VAR_FIND):\n- Found, a number or array element: BC is stored in it (AS_STORE).\n- Found, a string: a new copy of the text is made (NEW_STRING) and the old variable\n  removed.\n- Not found: the error is cancelled and a new variable added at the end of the\n  variables area: one letter (kind 011) or a long name (010, the last character with bit 7\n  set), then the value, low byte first; or a string (NEW_STRING). An array not made by\n  DIM is error code 1 (report 2).\nObserved: LET A=5 adds 66 05 00, LET AB=7 46 A7 07 00, LET A$=\"HI\" 86 2D 2E 01.","comment":"An error already: nothing"},
        "3138": {"comment":"Keep the value"},
        "3139": {"comment":"Find the variable named at DEST"},
        "3148": {"comment":"ERR_NR: code 2, a bad subscript?"},
        "3153": {"comment":"Carry: found ($FF)"},
        "3154": {"comment":"Z: a string"},
        "3158": {"comment":"Found"},
        "3160": {"comment":"Not found: cancel the error"},
        "3162": {"comment":"A new string"},
        "3164": {"comment":"A new number: the name's length"},
        "3170": {"synopsis":"BC = 2 + the name's length: the new variable's size.","comment":"One more"},
        "3178": {"comment":"'(': an array not made by DIM"},
        "3182": {"comment":"Room in the workspace; DE = its first byte"},
        "3183": {"comment":"No room"},
        "3186": {"comment":"HL = the name"},
        "3189": {"comment":"BC = the name's length - 1"},
        "3192": {"comment":"DE = one below the room (that byte becomes the kind byte)"},
        "3195": {"comment":"$40: one letter's kind bits, by XOR with the letter"},
        "3199": {"comment":"Copy the name but its last character"},
        "3201": {"comment":"The last character, with bit 7"},
        "3205": {"comment":"$60: a long name's"},
        "3207": {"synopsis":"Writes the kind byte and moves the end mark (NEW_VAR_END), then stores the value.","comment":"HL = the room"},
        "3211": {"comment":"DE = the value's high byte, before the $80"},
        "3213": {"synopsis":"Stores the value at DE (high byte) and DE - 1.","comment":"HL = the value"},
        "3219": {"synopsis":"An existing variable: a number is stored (AS_STORE); a string is made again.","comment":"A number"},
        "3222": {"comment":"The new text as a new variable"},
        "3225": {"comment":"The old one: its kind byte ..."},
        "3229": {"comment":"... its length ..."},
        "3232": {"comment":"... and remove it"},
        "3235": {"synopsis":"A new string variable from the text at the stacked address.","comment":"HL = the text"},
        "3236": {"synopsis":"Adds a string variable named at DEST with a copy of the text at HL (up to its '\"', $01):\nkind 100, the text, the '\"'.","comment":"Look for the '\"'"},
        "3241": {"synopsis":"BC = the text's length + 2 (the kind byte and the '\"').","comment":"The '\"'?"},
        "3247": {"comment":"Room in the workspace"},
        "3250": {"comment":"No room"},
        "3251": {"comment":"Copy the text, from its end down"},
        "3255": {"comment":"$A0: a string's kind bits"},
        "3257": {"synopsis":"Finishes a new variable built in the workspace: its kind byte (A XOR the first letter at\nDEST) goes where the $80 end mark was, any edit line before it is removed (CLEAR_TO_HL),\nand the $80 goes after it, with E_LINE = D_FILE.\nEntry: HL = the variable's second byte (the room's first), A = the kind bits.","comment":"DE = the second byte"},
        "3261": {"comment":"The kind byte"},
        "3264": {"comment":"Remove the edit line, if any, before the variable"},
        "3269": {"comment":"The kind byte, in place of the old $80"},
        "3273": {"comment":"E_LINE = D_FILE: an empty workspace"},
        "3277": {"comment":"The end mark after the variable"},
        "3280": {"synopsis":"No such variable: error code 1 (report 2).","comment":"Drop"},
        "3283": {"synopsis":"DIM v(n) (class 0; runs only when running): n above 255 is error code 2 (report 3). Adds an\narray at the end of the variables area (NEW_VAR_END): kind 101, the size byte n and n + 1\nwords of 0, 2n + 4 bytes in all.\nObserved: DIM C(2) added A8 02 and 6 zero bytes; DIM D(256) gave report 3.","comment":"A = B: n's high byte"},
        "3287": {"comment":"Keep n"},
        "3292": {"comment":"BC = 2n + 4"},
        "3295": {"comment":"Room in the workspace"},
        "3305": {"comment":"Zero ..."},
        "3307": {"comment":"... the elements"},
        "3310": {"comment":"The size byte"},
        "3311": {"comment":"$80: an array's kind bits"},
        "3315": {"synopsis":"WORKSPACE_RST's work: opens BC bytes at D_FILE - 1 (MAKE_ROOM) and keeps E_LINE where it\nwas, so the bytes join the end of the workspace whether or not it was empty.","comment":"Keep E_LINE"},
        "3319": {"comment":"At D_FILE - 1"},
        "3329": {"comment":"E_LINE back"},
        "3334": {"comment":"HL = the last byte, DE = the first"},
        "3338": {"synopsis":"Removes everything from E_LINE up to D_FILE (RECLAIM_RANGE): the edit line, or what a\nrunning line left in the workspace.","comment":"HL = D_FILE"},
        "3341": {"synopsis":"Removes E_LINE up to HL - 1 (RECLAIM_RANGE).","comment":"DE = E_LINE"},
        "3348": {"synopsis":"Carry set if A is a letter ($26-$3F).","comment":"$26 and up?"},
        "3352": {"synopsis":"Carry set if A is a digit or a letter ($1C-$3F).","comment":"$1C and up?"},
        "3354": {"synopsis":"Returns with carry clear if A was below the lower bound; otherwise carry set if A is\nbelow $40.","comment":"Carry now: at or above the lower bound"},
        "3359": {"synopsis":"The operation routines, by code: 0 OP_SUBTRACT, 1 OP_ADD, 2 OP_MULTIPLY, 3 OP_DIVIDE,\n4 OP_AND, 5 OP_OR, 6 OP_POWER, 7 OP_EQUAL, 8 OP_GREATER, 9 OP_LESS; the string comparisons\n10 OP_STR_EQUAL, 11 OP_STR_GREATER, 12 OP_STR_LESS. EV_STACK runs one with HL = the left\noperand and DE = the right; HL is the result (EV_RESULT). True is -1, false 0."},
        "3385": {"synopsis":"HL = HL - DE; a signed overflow is error code 5 (report 6).","comment":"Clear carry"},
        "3390": {"synopsis":"HL = HL + DE; a signed overflow is error code 5 (report 6).","comment":"Clear carry"},
        "3393": {"synopsis":"Returns unless the add or subtract overflowed (P/V set).","comment":"No overflow: done"},
        "3394": {"synopsis":"Error code 5 (report 6): a result out of range, a division by 0, a negative power.\nObserved: 32767+1, 182*181, 1/0, 2**15, 2**-1."},
        "3396": {"synopsis":"HL = HL x DE: multiplies the sizes (MULT16) and gives the product the sign; a product\nabove 32767 is error code 5 (report 6).","comment":"HL, DE = the sizes; A = how many were negative"},
        "3400": {"comment":"Keep the count"},
        "3404": {"comment":"Too big"},
        "3406": {"synopsis":"Negates HL when the count of negative operands in A' is odd. Also OP_POWER's end."},
        "3407": {"comment":"A = the count"},
        "3408": {"comment":"Odd?"},
        "3410": {"comment":"Yes: negative"},
        "3413": {"synopsis":"BC:HL = HL x DE, unsigned 32 bits (shift and add, 16 steps; BC starts as the multiplier\nand gathers the high word as its bits shift out).\nExit: HL = the low word, BC = the high word; Z set if the product is below 32768 (BC = 0\nand bit 15 of HL clear). Also RND_FN's.","comment":"BC = the multiplier"},
        "3415": {"comment":"16 bits"},
        "3420": {"synopsis":"One bit: the product doubles; the multiplier's top bit decides the add.","comment":"HL x 2"},
        "3421": {"comment":"BC x 2, its top bit into the carry"},
        "3425": {"comment":"Bit clear: no add"},
        "3427": {"comment":"+ the multiplicand"},
        "3430": {"comment":"A carry goes into the high word"},
        "3431": {"synopsis":"The next bit."},
        "3434": {"comment":"Z: high word 0 and bit 15 clear"},
        "3440": {"synopsis":"HL = HL ** DE by repeated multiplying (x ** 0 = 1); negative when the base is negative and\nthe exponent odd. A negative exponent or a result above 32767 is error code 5 (report 6).\nObserved: 2**10 1024, -2**3 -8, 2**0 1.","comment":"A negative exponent"},
        "3444": {"comment":"A = 0, 1 if the base is negative ..."},
        "3448": {"comment":"... and stays 1 only for an odd exponent"},
        "3451": {"comment":"BC = the exponent"},
        "3453": {"comment":"DE = the base's size"},
        "3454": {"comment":"HL = 1"},
        "3457": {"synopsis":"Multiplies BC times, then gives the sign (MUL_SIGN).","comment":"Count down"},
        "3458": {"comment":"Past 0: done"},
        "3463": {"comment":"HL x the base"},
        "3467": {"comment":"Fits: on"},
        "3469": {"synopsis":"Too big: error code 5.","comment":"BC back"},
        "3472": {"synopsis":"HL = HL / DE, the quotient truncated toward 0 (a division of sizes, 16 steps). A divisor\nof 0 is error code 5 (report 6).\nThe sign bit rides at the dividend's bottom through the 16 shifts and comes out in the\ncarry at the end. The quotient bits come out inverted, so the INC HL makes -q: kept for a\nnegative result, negated otherwise.\nObserved: -7/2 -3, 7/-2 -3, -7/-2 3.","comment":"Dividing by 0?"},
        "3476": {"comment":"HL, DE = the sizes; A bit 0 = the sign"},
        "3480": {"comment":"The sign into the carry ..."},
        "3481": {"comment":"... and into the dividend's bottom; its top bit out"},
        "3483": {"comment":"A:C = the dividend"},
        "3485": {"comment":"HL = the remainder"},
        "3490": {"synopsis":"One step: the remainder takes the next bit and the divisor is taken off if it fits.","comment":"Remainder x 2 + the next bit"},
        "3492": {"comment":"Take the divisor off"},
        "3494": {"comment":"It fitted"},
        "3496": {"comment":"It did not: back"},
        "3497": {"synopsis":"The quotient bit (inverted: the borrow) in, the next dividend bit out.","comment":"Into C ..."},
        "3499": {"comment":"... and A"},
        "3502": {"comment":"HL = the inverted quotient"},
        "3504": {"comment":"+ 1: HL = -q"},
        "3506": {"comment":"The sign (carry) set: -q is right"},
        "3507": {"comment":"Otherwise q"},
        "3509": {"synopsis":"HL = HL AND DE, bit by bit (with true -1 and false 0 a logical AND).","comment":"High bytes"},
        "3516": {"synopsis":"HL = HL OR DE, bit by bit.","comment":"High bytes"},
        "3523": {"synopsis":"HL = -1 if HL = DE, else 0.","comment":"Compare"},
        "3526": {"synopsis":"HL = -1 on Z, else 0. Also the string = (OP_STR_EQUAL).","comment":"True ..."},
        "3530": {"comment":"... else 0"},
        "3532": {"synopsis":"HL = -1 if HL > DE (signed), else 0: OP_LESS with the operands swapped.","comment":"Swap"},
        "3533": {"synopsis":"HL = -1 if HL < DE (signed), else 0: the subtraction's sign, inverted when it\noverflowed. Also called from $0916.","comment":"HL - DE"},
        "3536": {"comment":"The sign into the carry"},
        "3538": {"comment":"No overflow: the sign is the answer"},
        "3541": {"comment":"Overflow: the other way"},
        "3542": {"synopsis":"HL = -1 if the carry is set, else 0. Also the string comparisons'.","comment":"HL = -carry"},
        "3545": {"synopsis":"The string =: -1 if the texts at HL and DE are the same (STR_COMPARE).","comment":"Compare"},
        "3550": {"synopsis":"The string <: -1 if the text at HL comes before the one at DE.","comment":"Swap: DE = the left operand"},
        "3551": {"synopsis":"The string >: -1 if the text at HL comes after the one at DE.","comment":"Compare"},
        "3556": {"synopsis":"Compares the texts at DE and HL by character codes, to the first difference or to their\nend. The '\"' ($01) that ends a text compares as a character, so a shorter text comes first\nunless the longer goes on with a space ($00): \"A \" < \"A\" is true (observed).\nExit: Z set if they are the same; otherwise carry set if DE's character is the smaller.","comment":"Compare a character"},
        "3558": {"comment":"Different: the carry says which is smaller"},
        "3559": {"comment":"Both $01: the end, the same"},
        "3565": {"synopsis":"HL = |HL|, DE = |DE|, A = how many of them were negative (0-2); bit 0 is the sign of a\nproduct or quotient.","comment":"A counts"},
        "3566": {"comment":"DE's size first; then falls in for HL's"},
        "3569": {"synopsis":"Swaps HL and DE, then ABS_HL.","comment":"Swap"},
        "3570": {"synopsis":"HL = |HL|, A + 1 if HL was negative. ABS's routine (FUNCTION_TABLE). -32768 stays as it is.\nObserved: ABS(-3) 3, ABS(-32767) 32767.","comment":"Negative?"},
        "3573": {"comment":"Count it"},
        "3574": {"synopsis":"HL = -HL (complement and add 1); A and the flags kept.","comment":"Keep AF"},
        "3584": {"synopsis":"The character set: 64 patterns of 8 bytes, codes $00-$3F in order, one byte per pixel row,\nbit 7 the leftmost pixel. The ULA reads a row at (I & $FE) x 256 + code x 8 + the line, with\nI = $0E (RAM_FILL); a set bit is ink, and a code with bit 7 set (inverse) shows the pattern\nthe other way round (zx8081-ula.c, zx8081-video.c). Klive's ZX80_CHARSET names them.\nThe ROM's last 512 bytes, $0E00-$0FFF.\n$00 - Character: ' '"},
        "3592": {"synopsis":"$01 - Character: '\"'"},
        "3600": {"synopsis":"$02 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3608": {"synopsis":"$03 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3616": {"synopsis":"$04 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3624": {"synopsis":"$05 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3632": {"synopsis":"$06 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3640": {"synopsis":"$07 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3648": {"synopsis":"$08 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3656": {"synopsis":"$09 - Character: mosaic"},
        "3664": {"synopsis":"$0A - Character: mosaic"},
        "3672": {"synopsis":"$0B - Character: mosaic"},
        "3680": {"synopsis":"$0C - Character: ukp"},
        "3688": {"synopsis":"$0D - Character: '$'"},
        "3696": {"synopsis":"$0E - Character: ':'"},
        "3704": {"synopsis":"$0F - Character: '?'"},
        "3712": {"synopsis":"$10 - Character: '('"},
        "3720": {"synopsis":"$11 - Character: ')'"},
        "3728": {"synopsis":"$12 - Character: '-'"},
        "3736": {"synopsis":"$13 - Character: '+'"},
        "3744": {"synopsis":"$14 - Character: '*'"},
        "3752": {"synopsis":"$15 - Character: '/'"},
        "3760": {"synopsis":"$16 - Character: '='"},
        "3768": {"synopsis":"$17 - Character: '>'"},
        "3776": {"synopsis":"$18 - Character: '<'"},
        "3784": {"synopsis":"$19 - Character: ';'"},
        "3792": {"synopsis":"$1A - Character: ','"},
        "3800": {"synopsis":"$1B - Character: '.'"},
        "3808": {"synopsis":"$1C - Character: '0'"},
        "3816": {"synopsis":"$1D - Character: '1'"},
        "3824": {"synopsis":"$1E - Character: '2'"},
        "3832": {"synopsis":"$1F - Character: '3'"},
        "3840": {"synopsis":"$20 - Character: '4'"},
        "3848": {"synopsis":"$21 - Character: '5'"},
        "3856": {"synopsis":"$22 - Character: '6'"},
        "3864": {"synopsis":"$23 - Character: '7'"},
        "3872": {"synopsis":"$24 - Character: '8'"},
        "3880": {"synopsis":"$25 - Character: '9'"},
        "3888": {"synopsis":"$26 - Character: 'A'"},
        "3896": {"synopsis":"$27 - Character: 'B'"},
        "3904": {"synopsis":"$28 - Character: 'C'"},
        "3912": {"synopsis":"$29 - Character: 'D'"},
        "3920": {"synopsis":"$2A - Character: 'E'"},
        "3928": {"synopsis":"$2B - Character: 'F'"},
        "3936": {"synopsis":"$2C - Character: 'G'"},
        "3944": {"synopsis":"$2D - Character: 'H'"},
        "3952": {"synopsis":"$2E - Character: 'I'"},
        "3960": {"synopsis":"$2F - Character: 'J'"},
        "3968": {"synopsis":"$30 - Character: 'K'"},
        "3976": {"synopsis":"$31 - Character: 'L'"},
        "3984": {"synopsis":"$32 - Character: 'M'"},
        "3992": {"synopsis":"$33 - Character: 'N'"},
        "4000": {"synopsis":"$34 - Character: 'O'"},
        "4008": {"synopsis":"$35 - Character: 'P'"},
        "4016": {"synopsis":"$36 - Character: 'Q'"},
        "4024": {"synopsis":"$37 - Character: 'R'"},
        "4032": {"synopsis":"$38 - Character: 'S'"},
        "4040": {"synopsis":"$39 - Character: 'T'"},
        "4048": {"synopsis":"$3A - Character: 'U'"},
        "4056": {"synopsis":"$3B - Character: 'V'"},
        "4064": {"synopsis":"$3C - Character: 'W'"},
        "4072": {"synopsis":"$3D - Character: 'X'"},
        "4080": {"synopsis":"$3E - Character: 'Y'"},
        "4088": {"synopsis":"$3F - Character: 'Z'"}
      }
    }
  },
  "provenance": {
